-- ============================================================================
-- PD8 EXPERT DEEP SEED: Well Intervention, advanced tier.
--
-- Six modules, 26 lessons, 132 questions (15 per module bank plus a 42
-- question final exam). The structure row is GENERATED from the tier's
-- manifest.json rather than hand typed.
--
-- What this tier owns: WHAT THE FIT LEFT OUT. Every one of its six graded
-- fields is a return of the SAME functions the Professional tier called, run
-- either on the samples the classifier DISCARDED, or at the dial settings
-- where its verdict FLIPS, or on a column that was NEVER FILLED IN, or PAST
-- the plausibility limit the module's own refusal text names. Nothing new is
-- introduced anywhere in this tier. That is deliberate and it is the whole
-- shape of the argument: the tier is the Professional tier's calls re-run at
-- the edges, and if a single new function were needed to reach these findings
-- they would be a different module's problem instead of this one's.
--
-- THE ORACLE RECORDS NONE OF IT, AND THAT IS THE STARTING POINT. Both of the
-- oracle's checks belong to the Associate tier, both are genuinely independent,
-- and both hold to machine precision. Then it stops. Its golden publishes four
-- labelled histories with a late derivative slope for each, five skin pairs,
-- one geometry floor and one power law, and NO expected mechanism, confidence,
-- verdict, refusal or block reason. `chanDiagnosis`, `screenTreatments`,
-- `rankTreatments` and `skinFromPiRatio` are not asserted against anything at
-- all. Every finding in this tier therefore lives in that SILENCE, and one of
-- them is silence by construction: the oracle's own late-slope helper applies
-- the SAME positive-derivative filter the engine does, and not one of its four
-- histories changes sign in the late window, so the filter could not be caught
-- by the referee even in principle.
--
-- THE CENTRE OF THE TIER. THE CONTRARY EVIDENCE IS FILTERED OUT BEFORE THE
-- FIT, AND THE COUNT OF IT IS COMPUTED AND THEN DISCARDED. `chanDiagnosis`
-- builds its derivative fit from the late samples whose derivative is
-- positive, so a ratio that has TURNED BACK DOWN after a rate cut is read
-- entirely on the samples from before the turn. The engine KNOWS: two lines
-- later it computes `negativeDerivatives`, and it reads that count only inside
-- the `!derFit.ok` branch and the flat branch. Whenever three positive samples
-- survive, `derFit.ok` is true, the count is thrown away, and the note it
-- carries, "A ratio that has turned back down is itself the coning signature,
-- but confirm it against the plot rather than on this alone", is UNREACHABLE.
-- On the teaching well the discarded stretch fitted on its own has a fit
-- quality of 0.999955540, cleaner than the 0.998513658 the engine reported for
-- the verdict it actually gave, and its ratio slope is -0.749171775: a rate cut
-- followed by a falling water-oil ratio, which is the coning field test and the
-- coning answer. The module's own header says channelling and coning need
-- OPPOSITE treatments and that recommending the wrong one is money down a
-- hole. A history can argue both and the engine will report only the treatable
-- one. The digest's falling-only demonstration is how a lesson shows the note
-- the engine would have printed, by removing every positive sample so the
-- unreachable branch finally fires.
--
-- AND IT COMPOUNDS, BECAUSE DROPPING THE SAMPLES ALSO SHORTENS THE SPAN.
-- `spanDecades` is computed on the fit, so the dropped points cost log time as
-- well as evidence, and the span has its own gate at `minSpanDecades` 0.4. On
-- the teaching well the loss is 0.257320134 of a log cycle at every window
-- where it can be measured, and at `lateFraction` 0.30 the reported span is
-- 0.450310235 against that gate: the reading clears refusal by 0.050310235 of
-- a log cycle, and it would have cleared by 0.307630369 had nothing been
-- dropped. On a short enough window the drop is the difference between a
-- reading and a refusal.
--
-- THE DIAL MOVES THE ANSWER BY MORE THAN THE MARGIN THAT DECIDES THE MONEY.
-- `lateFraction` is a free analyst choice with a default of 0.5, no guidance
-- anywhere, no sweep helper and nothing in the return naming its effect. On the
-- teaching well, across the dial and with NOT ONE DATUM CHANGED, the derivative
-- slope moves by 0.370920348, from 1.229355999 to 1.600276347, and the water
-- shutoff comes back a candidate on one window and blocked on another. It is
-- also SILENTLY CLAMPED to 0.1 through 1.0, which is documented nowhere: hand
-- in a negative fraction and the engine reads the last tenth of the history and
-- returns coning, with no note that the request was replaced.
--
-- AND THE TOP END OF THAT RANGE IS TAKEN FROM A READING THE ENGINE REFUSED.
-- SAY SO WHEREVER THE RANGE IS QUOTED. At `lateFraction` 0.20 the span gate
-- fires: the engine returns `mechanism: indeterminate` with no `spanDecades`
-- key at all AND WITH `derivativeSlope` AND `derivativeR2` FULLY POPULATED, at
-- 1.600276347, the steepest derivative slope anywhere in the sweep, off a
-- window it declined to read. The `!derFit.ok` branch immediately above nulls
-- both of those fields; the span branch does not. So the two refusals in one
-- function have different return shapes, and a consumer reading
-- `derivativeSlope` without first reading `mechanism` gets the engine's
-- steepest number off its least trustworthy window. A tier that quoted the
-- range without that sentence would be laundering a refusal into a headline.
--
-- THE CLEANEST FAILS-OPEN IN THE MODULE, AND IT IS ONE JAVASCRIPT SPELLING.
-- `Number(null)` is 0. So is `Number('')` and `Number([])`. Only
-- `Number(undefined)` is NaN. `chanDiagnosis` filters its input on a finite
-- time and a finite ratio and DOES NOT REQUIRE A FINITE DERIVATIVE, so a
-- history exported with the Bourdet derivative column never computed satisfies
-- the flat test at every late point, takes the FLAT BRANCH, and comes back
-- ok true, `mechanism: displacement`, confidence n/a, with the sentence
-- "Nothing is changing, so there is no mechanism to diagnose and nothing on
-- this well for an intervention to fix. That is a finding, not a failure to
-- reach one." Spell the same missing column `undefined` and the same history
-- comes back `indeterminate` with an honest refusal. Same missing data,
-- opposite answers, and THE REASSURING ONE IS THE SPELLING EVERY JSON EXPORT
-- AND EVERY SQL NULL PRODUCES. On the teaching gas history, whose ratio climbs
-- by a factor of 2.248088575 across the window the engine calls flat, that is
-- the answer returned on the very fluid the module's own gas reasoning sends
-- the user to. The fix is one clause: require a finite derivative in `clean`,
-- or count how many rows carried one at all and refuse the flat branch when
-- the answer is none.
--
-- AND THE FLAT BRANCH ASSERTS SOMETHING IT NEVER CHECKED. Its note opens "The
-- ratio is sitting flat at X" and nothing in that branch looks at the ratio:
-- the condition is entirely about the derivative, and the SAME RETURN OBJECT
-- carries a ratio slope and fit quality that say the opposite. On the teaching
-- gas history the sentence names a ratio sitting flat while the object beside
-- it carries `worSlope` 0.356090047 at `worR2` 0.949579198, on 26 clean
-- samples. The engine contradicts itself inside one object. The published flat
-- history is the ONE series where the sentence happens to be true, which is
-- exactly why nobody noticed: the only case anybody tested it on really was
-- flat.
--
-- A GUARD THAT FIRES BY ACCIDENT, WHICH IS WORSE THAN ONE THAT NEVER FIRES.
-- `logLogSlope` computes the fit quality as `syy > 0 ? ordinary : 1`, intending
-- to hand back a perfect fit when every y is identical. AN EARLIER READING OF
-- THIS SAID THE BRANCH NEVER FIRES AND THAT WAS WRONG. Whether the sum of
-- squares reaches EXACTLY zero over identical y depends on the sample count AND
-- the value TOGETHER, and the digest sweeps both: it fires at n = 3 and n = 4 on
-- every value tried, splits at n = 5, mostly stops from n = 8, and y = 2 keeps
-- firing until n = 32 where it stops. There is no rule a caller could hold in
-- their head. A guard that never fires is dead code and dead code is harmless
-- once you know it is dead; THIS one fires on small tidy cases and stops firing
-- on real ones, which is the pattern that survives a test suite and fails in
-- production. The four-point demonstration at y = 5 fires and returns the
-- intended perfect fit. The twenty-sample constant-derivative history, a ratio
-- rising exactly logarithmically, does NOT fire, comes back at a fit quality of
-- 2.271680e-31, falls under `minR2`, and is refused with "the fit explains only
-- 0.0 percent of it. Reading a mechanism off this would be reading noise", on
-- data with no scatter whatever.
--
-- THE SKIN GUARD SITS AT THE SINGULARITY AND ITS REFUSAL ADVERTISES A
-- DIFFERENT LIMIT. `skinPiMultiplier` refuses only when the denominator reaches
-- ZERO, and the refusal text then says "Real treatments reach about -3 to -5 on
-- acid and -5 to -6 on a fracture; ask for less." EVERYTHING BETWEEN IS
-- ACCEPTED IN SILENCE, with no warning, no note and no flag. On the teaching
-- geometry the multiplier at an after-skin of -5 is 6.292734624 and at -6.8 it
-- is 26.457156986, a factor of 4.204397383 handed back with the same confidence
-- as the honest one. `minimumSkin` is computed on every call and returned
-- INSIDE the successful result, and is never compared against anything but
-- zero. One line comparing the requested skin against the value already sitting
-- in the return would close it. And the refusal, when it finally comes,
-- compares a number against itself: on the teaching geometry the floor is
-- -7.361728083 and a request of -7.400 prints "Taking the skin to -7.4 would
-- put it below the -7.4 this geometry allows", because both numbers are printed
-- to one decimal. A learner reading that sentence cannot tell what was refused.
--
-- THE SCREENING DOES NOT KNOW WHICH FLUID IT READ. `screenTreatments` reads
-- only `mechanism.id`, and nothing in `chanDiagnosis`'s return says whether it
-- read a water-oil ratio or a gas-oil ratio, while the module's own gas
-- reasoning ends on the instruction to "Run the diagnostic on the gas-oil ratio
-- before deciding". Hand the screening the GAS diagnosis and the WATER shutoff
-- comes back BLOCKED, with its reasons quoting the water cut. That block is
-- issued on gas evidence, on a well row that did not change. That one sentence
-- is this tier's fails-open and this tier's fluid blindness in a single line.
--
-- TWO SMALLER ONES, KEPT BECAUSE THEY SHOW THE SAME HABIT.
--
--   THE RATIO GATE IS UNIT BLIND AND READS ONE SAMPLE. `minWor` is 0.1, named
--   and documented for a water-oil ratio, and applied unchanged to whatever
--   ratio column arrives, so on a gas-oil ratio in scf/stb it is inert by four
--   orders of magnitude. It is also applied to the LAST SAMPLE ALONE, so one
--   low final reading, a test after a shut-in or a bad meter, short-circuits
--   the entire diagnosis with no slope computed and no confidence returned.
--   The digest's demonstration is a climbing history whose one replaced final
--   sample turns a readable derivative slope of 1.150000000 into no reading at
--   all.
--
--   AND A TEST THAT DECIDES NOTHING. The hydraulic fracture verdict is a
--   ternary with the SAME STRING on both arms, so the skin test it performs has
--   never decided anything, and it reads as though somebody meant a different
--   word on the true arm. It is worth showing beside the three skin-gated
--   verdicts the same function writes CORRECTLY, because the point is that the
--   module knows how to write this branch and wrote it properly three times.
--
-- WHAT THE TIER ENDS KNOWING, AND IT IS ONE SENTENCE. A DIAGNOSIS IS A READING
-- OF A WINDOW, AND BOTH THE READING AND THE WINDOW ARE CHOSEN BY THE ANALYST.
-- The fit quality is a statement about the line rather than about the well, the
-- samples the fit declined to draw are the ones arguing for the other
-- mechanism, and the threshold the verdict turns on separates two pictures the
-- module's own comment says cannot be separated by their slopes. None of that
-- makes the module useless. It makes every number it returns conditional on a
-- choice, and naming the choice beside the number is the whole of what an
-- expert reading is.
-- ============================================================================

insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
values ('intervention', 'advanced', '{"modules":[{"key":"m01-the-evidence-that-argues-the-other-way","title":"The Evidence That Argues the Other Way","lesson_keys":["l01-the-filter-that-drops-a-sign","l02-a-count-computed-and-discarded","l03-fitting-the-discarded-stretch","l04-the-span-the-drop-costs","l05-what-an-unreachable-note-means"]},{"key":"m02-the-dial-that-decides-the-spend","title":"The Dial That Decides the Spend","lesson_keys":["l01-one-history-many-windows","l02-where-the-verdict-flips","l03-the-margin-against-the-threshold","l04-a-clamp-documented-nowhere","l05-what-a-sweep-cannot-settle"]},{"key":"m03-a-missing-column-read-as-zero","title":"A Missing Column Read as Zero","lesson_keys":["l01-the-spellings-of-no-value","l02-the-flat-branch","l03-a-branch-that-asserts-what-it-never-checked","l04-what-ok-means-here"]},{"key":"m04-guards-in-the-wrong-place","title":"Guards in the Wrong Place","lesson_keys":["l01-the-guard-at-the-singularity","l02-a-refusal-that-names-another-limit","l03-a-guard-that-fires-by-accident","l04-clean-data-refused-as-noise","l05-one-sample-that-ends-a-reading"]},{"key":"m05-what-the-screening-cannot-know","title":"What the Screening Cannot Know","lesson_keys":["l01-it-does-not-know-the-fluid","l02-confidence-read-in-one-place","l03-a-test-that-decides-nothing","l04-a-verdict-with-no-golden"]},{"key":"m06-the-expert-reading","title":"The Expert Reading","lesson_keys":["l01-the-story-so-far","l02-working-the-capstone","l03-onward"]}]}'::jsonb, 1)
on conflict (app_slug, tier) do update
  set structure = excluded.structure,
      content_version = excluded.content_version,
      active = true,
      updated_at = now();

delete from public.academy_quiz_questions
 where app_slug = 'intervention' and tier = 'advanced';

insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-evidence-that-argues-the-other-way', 1, 'Teaching well ELELENWO-4 opens a late window of 19 samples at the engine default lateFraction of 0.5. How many of them reach the least squares that produces derivativeSlope, and what records the removal?', '["All 19 reach it, because a window is the reading and logLogSlope takes the logarithm of the magnitude of a negative y, which is how the falling stretch fits at a slope of -0.850000000000.", "15 reach it, and the 4 that were removed come back in a field of their own so that a caller can refit them against coningSlope.", "15 reach it, and nothing records the removal: the n that comes back is the count left after clean.filter(p => p.derivative > 0) and after logLogSlope drops any remaining y that is not strictly positive.", "All 34 positive samples in the history reach it, because the derivative fit ignores lateFraction and reads the raw series while only the ratio fit is windowed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Two filters point the same way and neither announces itself, so the 15 is a count after the drop and the 4 negative samples leave without a trace.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-evidence-that-argues-the-other-way', 2, 'A derived demonstration hands logLogSlope six points of which two are negative. It returns ok = true, n = 4, a slope of 1.000000000 and spanDecades of 0.903089987. What stretch is that span the length of?', '["The stretch the four surviving points cover, which is 0.602059991 of a log cycle shorter than the 1.505149978 the six points were handed in across.", "The stretch the six points cover, since the span is read off the first and last times supplied and the positivity filter touches only the fitted values.", "The vertical range of the four surviving y values in log cycles, which is why it comes out below the range of times.", "The stretch covered by the two points that were dropped, published so that a caller can subtract it and recover the window."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The fit reports 0.903089987 against 1.505149978 handed in, and says nothing at all about the two points it removed.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-evidence-that-argues-the-other-way', 3, 'chanDiagnosis computes negativeDerivatives two lines after the derivative fit. On which paths is that count actually read?', '["Against ambiguousBand of 0.25, before confidence is assigned, since a window that dropped samples is by definition a borderline reading.", "In the note builder for every branch, so that a channelling verdict carries a line naming how many samples were set aside.", "Against minSpanDecades of 0.4, as the correction that adds the dropped stretch back and turns the surviving span into the window span.", "Inside the branch that fires when the derivative fit failed, and inside the flat branch, and nowhere else."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Whenever three positive samples survive the filter, derFit.ok is true, neither of those two branches runs, and the count of 4 is dropped on the floor.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-evidence-that-argues-the-other-way', 4, 'Why can the sentence about a ratio that has turned back down never print on teaching well ELELENWO-4, which is the history that has turned back down?', '["The note is gated on coningSlope of -0.1 and the reported derivative slope of 1.442132492 sits far above it.", "The four falling samples still leave 15 rising ones, so the derivative fit succeeds, and the note is attached to a count that only the failed-fit branch and the flat branch ever read.", "The note prints only when ambiguous comes back false, and this reading is 0.142132492 from the boundary and therefore inside the band.", "The note requires a span above minSpanDecades of 0.4 and the four falling samples cover only 0.192990101 log cycles."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Enough contrary evidence to lose the fit gets the warning; four samples out of 19 get nothing, and that is the harder case.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-evidence-that-argues-the-other-way', 5, 'The four samples ELELENWO-4 discards fit on their own to a ratio slope of -0.749171775150 at a fit quality of 0.999955540052 as a fraction. Why is that not by itself grounds to overturn the channelling verdict?', '["Because -0.749171775150 fails to clear coningSlope, which sits at -0.1, so the discarded stretch does not read as coning either.", "Because 0.999955540052 is below the 0.998513658 the engine reported, so the line the engine drew is the tighter of the two.", "Four samples spanning 0.192990101 log cycles would not clear a minSpanDecades of 0.4 as a window in their own right, so the stretch is proof that a second verdict is in the history rather than proof of which verdict is right.", "Because logLogSlope refuses any call built on fewer than six producing samples and would not have fitted those four at all."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both fits are excellent and they point opposite ways, and a fit quality of 0.999955540052 is a statement about a line rather than about a well.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-evidence-that-argues-the-other-way', 6, 'On the derived span-loss sweep the reported spanDecades falls short of the window by 0.257320134 of a log cycle at lateFraction 0.30 and again at 0.35. Why is the loss the same on both rows?', '["The same four falling samples leave at both fractions, so the fit stops at the same last rising sample and gives up the same stretch of plot each time.", "Because the loss is fixed at minSpanDecades of 0.4 less the width of the ambiguous band, which is a constant of the defaults rather than of the history.", "Because spanDecades is rounded up to the nearest window length in each case, so the difference between the reported span and the window is a fixed rounding step of the log scale.", "Because the two windows contain the same 19 late samples and differ only in the time the window opens."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At 0.30 the window runs 0.707630369 log cycles and the fit reports 0.450310235, and at 0.35 it runs 0.836290436 and reports 0.578970302.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-evidence-that-argues-the-other-way', 7, 'At lateFraction 0.30 on ELELENWO-4 the reported span of 0.450310235 clears minSpanDecades by 0.050310235 while the drop itself cost 0.257320134. What does that comparison establish?', '["That the span gate is generous, because a margin of 0.050310235 is five times the width a log-log plot can resolve.", "That the reading survives its own span gate on about a fifth of what the filter took, so a slightly shorter window would be refused for a span the filter shortened.", "That minSpanDecades is the wrong gate for this history and the reading should have been refused on minR2 of 0.5 instead.", "That the four dropped samples cannot have mattered to the reading, since the reported span clears the gate without them and the mechanism still came back channelling on a clean line."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The loss of 0.257320134 is more than half the whole gate of 0.4, so the filter decides span questions the gate is then blamed for.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-evidence-that-argues-the-other-way', 8, 'In one return object at lateFraction 0.5 the ratio fit used n = 19 over 1.157940604 log cycles and the derivative fit used n = 15 over 0.900620470. What does the field named spanDecades describe?', '["The ratio fit, which is the one taken over every late sample and therefore the one a window length belongs to.", "The window itself, running 1.157940604 log cycles, which is why the two slopes can be compared against one another.", "The overlap of the two fits, which is the stretch both of them sit on and the only stretch either slope is licensed over.", "The derivative fit alone, which is 0.257320134 of a log cycle shorter than the window it is reported beside."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The two slopes come back side by side as 1.040602176 and 1.442132492, a gap of 0.401530316, with nothing in the object saying they were measured on different data.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-evidence-that-argues-the-other-way', 9, 'A teaching demonstration of 8 samples, every one carrying a negative derivative, returns ok = true, mechanism coning at confidence low, worSlope 0.143809349 and worR2 0.063449269. What made the coning branch reachable there?', '["The ratio slope of 0.143809349 fell below coningSlope of -0.1 and selected the coning label directly.", "A worR2 of 0.063449269 sits far under minR2 of 0.5, and a ratio fit that poor routes the reading to coning as the module''s default label.", "No positive sample survived the filter, so derFit failed and the branch that reads the negative count ran instead.", "The window opened at t = 1276.416078 days, which is past the turn, and chanDiagnosis reads a post-turn window as coning without fitting anything."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The count of negatives is read only where the fit could not be made, and 8 negative samples out of 8 is the one arrangement that gets it read.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-evidence-that-argues-the-other-way', 10, 'The golden publishes four labelled histories of 40 samples each. Why can none of them exercise the line that reads negativeDerivatives?', '["Three of them produce 20 positive late samples and no negative ones at all, and the fourth produces 20 exactly zero, so the count is 0 wherever it is read and 0 wherever it is not.", "Because the oracle reads all four of them on a window that starts at t = 186.345364 days, which is a different window from the one the default lateFraction of 0.5 opens, so the count is taken over other samples.", "Because the golden publishes a lateDerivativeSlope for each and the engine short-circuits to that published value.", "Because their spans of 2.477121255 log cycles are too long for the count to be assembled."]'::jsonb, 0, 'No published case makes that line of code do anything, which is why a count computed and discarded survived review.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-evidence-that-argues-the-other-way', 11, 'A report quotes derivativeR2 of 0.998513658 as the evidence that the channelling verdict on ELELENWO-4 is settled. What is wrong with that argument?', '["Nothing is wrong with it as far as it goes, but 0.998513658 should be quoted as a percentage because minR2 of 0.5 is held in that scale.", "A fit quality grades the line, not the well, and the stretch the engine excluded fits at 0.999955540052 as a fraction while arguing for the opposite mechanism.", "The number belongs to the ratio fit rather than to the derivative fit, so it grades worSlope of 1.040602176 over 19 samples and not the slope of 1.442132492 the verdict actually used.", "It is below minR2 of 0.5 once the four dropped samples are added back, so the reading would not have survived the gate."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both lines are excellent and they disagree, so 0.998513658 arbitrates nothing about which mechanism the history holds.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-evidence-that-argues-the-other-way', 12, 'logLogSlope refuses two points with the words that a slope needs at least three points that are both positive. What refusal covers a fit that lost its contrary samples to the filter?', '["The minSpanDecades gate at 0.4, which is the refusal written for exactly that loss and fires whenever the stretch that survived the filter falls short of the window it came from.", "The ambiguous flag, which comes back true whenever the count of dropped samples is above zero and is the module''s standing warning that a fit lost evidence.", "The message that every point is at the same time, which is the refusal covering a window that lost its tail.", "There is none. Nothing in the module refuses, warns or notes when the filter removed evidence, and the samples leave with no field recording them."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The module refuses only when too little survives to fit at all, and on this window 15 samples survived.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-evidence-that-argues-the-other-way', 13, 'ELELENWO-4 peaks at a water-oil ratio of 14.587294415 at t = 1990.592514 days and ends at 9.329979637 at t = 3600.000000 days. What does the engine make of the fall between them at the default window?', '["It uses none of it: every sample past the peak carries a negative derivative, all four are filtered out, and the reported derivativeSlope of 1.442132492 stops at the last rising sample.", "It fits the fall along with the climb and reports the net slope of 1.040602176, which is why the ratio slope is so much flatter than the derivative slope.", "It reads the fall as a coning signature and lowers the confidence to low, which is why ambiguous comes back true on this reading.", "It reports the fall inside spanDecades of 0.900620470, which is the stretch from the window start to the last sample in the history."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The peak is the last sample before the well was beaned back on day 2200, and the four samples after it are exactly what the fit never sees.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-evidence-that-argues-the-other-way', 14, 'The magnitude of the falling derivative over ELELENWO-4''s four discarded samples fits at a slope of -0.850000000000 with a fit quality of 1.000000000000. What is that number worth to the diagnosis?', '["It is reported as derivativeSlope with its sign restored, which is why the reading comes back at confidence low.", "It is compared against coningSlope of -0.1 inside the flat branch, which is the comparison that would have raised coning on this history if that branch had ever been reached.", "Nothing, because chanDiagnosis never fits it: the samples are removed before the fit and there is no field in the return object for a slope taken on them.", "It sets spanDecades to 0.192990101, the stretch those four samples cover, which is why the reported span of 0.900620470 falls short of the window it names."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A fit quality of 1.000000000000 on the discarded stretch is a fact about the history that the return object has no way to carry.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-evidence-that-argues-the-other-way', 15, 'A reader is handed derivativeSlope 1.442132492 and derivativeR2 0.998513658 and nothing else from an ELELENWO-4 run. Which of these can they not recover?', '["The window start, since lateFromT of 250.242976 days is computed by the caller from the fraction rather than returned inside the object.", "How many samples the window held before the filter, and therefore that 4 of them were falling and were removed.", "The mechanism, since the label is derived from the slope by the caller against channellingSlope of 1.3 rather than returned by chanDiagnosis.", "The threshold the slope was compared against, since channellingSlope of 1.3 is computed from the history rather than held as a default."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The return object carries the count after the drop and never the count before it, so 15 is visible and the 4 are not.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-dial-that-decides-the-spend', 1, 'The course quotes a derivative slope range of 0.370920348 on teaching well ELELENWO-4, running from 1.229355999 to 1.600276347. Where does the top of that range come from?', '["From lateFraction 0.20, a window the engine declined to read: it returned mechanism indeterminate with no spanDecades key at all, and handed the slope back fully populated anyway.", "From lateFraction 0.30, the narrowest window that still returns a mechanism, which is why the range stops there rather than at a refusal.", "From the full-history derivative fit over all 34 positive samples, which is the steepest reading because it spans 2.122891107 log cycles.", "From the clamp floor at 0.10, where the window opens at t = 2308.407093 days on the falling samples alone."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The span gate returns indeterminate with derivativeSlope and derivativeR2 still filled in, so 1.600276347 is the steepest number in the sweep and it comes off a refused reading.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-dial-that-decides-the-spend', 2, 'Between which two neighbouring rows of the derived window sweep does the water shutoff verdict on ELELENWO-4 turn from candidate into blocked?', '["Between 0.20 and 0.30, where the mechanism moves from indeterminate to channelling and the squeeze first becomes available.", "Between 0.50 and 0.60, where the margin against the threshold of 1.3 halves from 0.142132492 to 0.087035000 on a widening window.", "Between 0.70 and 0.80, where the derivative slope falls from 1.336892539 to 1.292632524 across a channellingSlope of 1.3.", "Between 0.90 and 1.00, where the derivative slope reaches its flattest value anywhere in the sweep, 1.229355999 over the whole 38 sample history."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nothing about the well changes across the sweep, and one dial step from 1.336892539 to 1.292632524 turns a treatment from a candidate into a categorical refusal.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-dial-that-decides-the-spend', 3, 'The narrowest margin anywhere in the ELELENWO-4 sweep is at lateFraction 0.80, where the derivative slope less channellingSlope is -0.007367476. What does a margin that size mean for the reading?', '["That the engine flags the row for review, because a margin under a tenth of the ambiguous band of 0.25 is what puts a reading onto the low confidence path, and every row of this sweep comes back low.", "That the block on the water shutoff rests on a distance no production measurement could resolve, and the engine returns it with the same certainty as any other verdict.", "That the reading is refused, since a slope that close to the threshold cannot be assigned to either picture.", "That the row should be read against coningSlope of -0.1 instead, because the margin has gone negative."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine never refuses on a small margin: it flags the band, returns displacement, and the screening blocks the squeeze on it.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-dial-that-decides-the-spend', 4, 'A user hands chanDiagnosis a lateFraction of 0.05 on ELELENWO-4, meaning the last handful of points. What comes back, and what tells them it was not the value they asked for?', '["A refusal, because the module counts before it reads and a fraction below the floor fails that count.", "A window opening at t = 15.000000 days and mechanism displacement, because a fraction below the floor is treated as a request for the whole 38 sample history.", "A window opening at t = 2308.407093 days and mechanism coning, together with a clamped flag on the returned object naming the value that was substituted.", "A window opening at t = 2308.407093 days and mechanism coning, and nothing tells them: the only clue is that 0.05 and 0.10 return identical answers."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The floor is 0.10 and the ceiling is 1.00, both silent, and coning appears nowhere in the derived sweep from 0.20 to 1.00 on this history.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-dial-that-decides-the-spend', 5, 'Why does the clamp floor on ELELENWO-4 return coning when no window in the sweep from 0.20 to 1.00 does?', '["The ratio over that window fits at a slope below coningSlope of -0.1, and the classifier reads the ratio slope when the window is short.", "The window at t = 2308.407093 days holds only the four samples past the choke, whose derivatives are all negative, so the fit fails and the branch that reads the negative count runs.", "Four samples is under the six the module needs, so it returns ok = false with the mechanism defaulted to coning.", "The window opens after the choke, and chanDiagnosis labels any post-event window coning without fitting anything."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That branch reads negativeDerivatives, which is 4 here, and the same count is discarded at every other fraction in the sweep.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-dial-that-decides-the-spend', 6, 'Both a lateFraction of 1.00 and a lateFraction of 2.50 return a derivative slope of 1.229355999 on ELELENWO-4. What does the second call actually do?', '["It refuses the call outright, because a fraction above one asks for more history than the 38 sample series holds, and the module counts before it reads.", "It pads the window with the earliest sample repeated, which is why the two slopes agree exactly.", "It reads the whole 38 sample history from t = 15.000000 days, because the request is trimmed to the ceiling with nothing saying it was trimmed.", "It reads the whole history and returns a warning naming the ceiling of 1.00, which is the one place in the module where the clamp is recorded on the returned object."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ask for more history than exists and you get the history, and 1.229355999 is the flattest reading in the sweep.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-dial-that-decides-the-spend', 7, 'Across the ELELENWO-4 sweep derivativeR2 falls from 0.999944740 to 0.994988494 while worR2 climbs from 0.063449269 to 0.989832434. What does an analyst who picks the window by fit quality get?', '["Either verdict they like: the derivative fit ranks the narrow window best, which says channelling and buys a squeeze, and the ratio fit ranks the wide window best, which says displacement and refuses it.", "The narrow window in both cases, since a fit over fewer samples over a shorter stretch always scores higher on both quantities.", "The wide window in both cases, since the two fit qualities are computed from the same sums and cannot disagree about ordering.", "A refusal, because the module compares the two fit qualities against each other and declines when they rank the windows in opposite orders."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The row at 0.20 pairs a derivativeR2 of 0.999944740 with a worR2 of 0.063449269, which is the sharpest form of that disagreement.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-dial-that-decides-the-spend', 8, 'Why is a higher derivativeR2 at lateFraction 0.20 not evidence that 0.20 was the right window?', '["Because 0.999944740 is quoted as a fraction while minR2 of 0.5 is held as a percentage in the error text, so the two numbers are not comparable at all.", "Because the row at 0.20 comes back at confidence low, and a low confidence reading disqualifies its fit quality from being compared with any other row.", "Because derivativeR2 at that row is measured on 4 positive samples while worR2 is measured on 8, so the two numbers describe different windows and neither describes the well.", "A fit quality says how well a line describes the points it was drawn through and nothing about whether those were the right points."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The narrow window fits beautifully because it holds few samples over a short stretch, and it still comes back indeterminate.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-dial-that-decides-the-spend', 9, 'At lateFraction 0.20 on ELELENWO-4 the derivative fit returns a slope of 1.600276347 at a fit quality of 0.999944740, and the mechanism comes back indeterminate. What refused it?', '["The fit quality, because 0.999944740 as a fraction is under the minR2 of 0.5 the defaults hold as a percentage.", "The span: the fit sat on 4 surviving samples and reported no spanDecades at all, so the reading failed the minSpanDecades gate of 0.4.", "The negative count, because 4 of the 8 late samples in that window were falling, and the classifier refuses any window whose samples are half negative.", "The ambiguous band, because 1.600276347 sits above the upper edge of 1.550000 and the reading falls outside the range the classifier is willing to label."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The window itself runs 0.450310235 log cycles, so it is the drop of 0.257320134 that puts the reported span under the gate.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-dial-that-decides-the-spend', 10, 'channellingSlope is 1.3 and ambiguousBand is 0.25. What range on the derivative slope does that put the ambiguous flag over, and what is the flag worth at the default window on ELELENWO-4?', '["1.050000 to 1.550000, and the flag is worth nothing at the default because 1.442132492 sits outside it.", "A band running from 1.3 upward to 1.550000, so the flag comes back true only above the threshold, which is where a squeeze is being recommended.", "1.050000 to 1.550000, a width of 0.500000, and at the default the reading sits 0.142132492 from the boundary, so ambiguous comes back true and the engine appends its own warning.", "1.050000 to 1.550000, and the flag suppresses the channelling verdict at the default window, which is why the reading returns at confidence low and the squeeze is only a candidate rather than a recommendation."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The band is a width of 0.500000 on a threshold of 1.3, and the margin at the default is 0.142132492, comfortably inside it.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-dial-that-decides-the-spend', 11, 'The published displacement history returns a derivative slope of exactly 1.000000000 at a fit quality of 1.000000000 and comes back at confidence high with ambiguous false. Why is that a warning about the flag rather than about the history?', '["Because a slope of one is the case the oracle''s own docstring calls the one that genuinely needs the plot and a person, and 1.000000000 sits outside the band from 1.050000 to 1.550000 so the flag says nothing about it.", "Because the history is a golden case and the flag is only computed on teaching series.", "Because a fit quality of 1.000000000 forces confidence to high regardless of where the slope sits against the band.", "Because the flag is read off worR2 rather than the derivative slope, and that history returns a worR2 of 1.000000000."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The band catches the reading at 0.142132492 from the boundary and misses the one at exactly 1.000000000, which is the harder case.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-dial-that-decides-the-spend', 12, 'The return object from chanDiagnosis carries lateFromT in days. What does it not carry about the window?', '["The start of the window, which has to be recovered by the caller from the sample times and the fraction they supplied.", "The count of late samples, which is why a reader cannot tell 19 samples at 0.50 from 23 samples at 0.60 on this teaching well.", "The span of the window in log cycles, which is reported only for the ratio fit and never for the derivative fit that produced the mechanism.", "The fraction that produced it, and any warning that a small change of fraction would change the mechanism."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The window start of 250.242976 days is recoverable and the dial setting of 0.5 that produced it is not, so a slope of 1.387035000 arrives with no way to say what it describes.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-dial-that-decides-the-spend', 13, 'Set the ELELENWO-4 sweep range of 0.370920348 against the margin of 0.142132492 the default reading carries. What does the comparison say?', '["That the margin is safe, because a range of 0.370920348 spread across nine dial settings averages well under the ambiguous band of 0.25 for any single step.", "That the analyst''s free choice of window is worth about two and a half times the distance the verdict rests on, so the dial outweighs the evidence.", "That the sweep should be averaged, since its midpoint is the reading least sensitive to the window.", "That the margin is measured on a different quantity from the range, so the two cannot be set against one another at all."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every row is the same 38 samples, so 0.370920348 of slope movement is the analyst and none of it is the well.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-dial-that-decides-the-spend', 14, 'The module does know how to refuse. Where does it count before it reads, and what does that guard have to say about lateFraction?', '["It refuses any fraction outside the range 0.1 to 1.0 with the same message it prints for a short history, on the grounds that both are argument checks.", "It refuses any window holding fewer than six late samples, which is the reason lateFraction 0.20 comes back indeterminate even though 8 late samples were in it.", "It refuses a history of fewer than six producing samples with ok = false, and it says nothing about lateFraction, which has no guard at all.", "It refuses a fraction of zero, and treats every other out of range value as a request for the default of 0.5."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The short history refusal names its want plainly, that six producing samples is the bare minimum, and the dial is answered silently at 0.05 and at 2.50 alike.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-dial-that-decides-the-spend', 15, 'The same ELELENWO-4 row screened at lateFraction 0.5 and again at 0.9 gives two rankings. What moves besides the water shutoff?', '["Recompletion, which falls from candidate to consider because it is a candidate when the mechanism is channelling or the skin is above 8 and this well is at 7.5.", "Matrix acid, which falls from candidate to marginal once the mechanism stops being channelling.", "Rate reduction, which rises from no to candidate on the displacement reading, because choking a well back is the answer to water arriving through the rock rather than through a path of its own.", "Gas shutoff, which falls from consider to blocked because the gas reasoning is gated on the water mechanism."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At 0.5 the order runs matrix acid, water shutoff and recompletion as candidates, and at 0.9 the water shutoff drops to blocked and recompletion to consider.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-a-missing-column-read-as-zero', 1, 'A production database exports a ratio history with the Bourdet derivative column never computed. Which spellings of that empty column survive into the flat test at Math.abs(p.derivative) < 1e-12?', '["Only the string 0, because the other spellings are non-numeric and the filter drops the sample before any test is reached on the value.", "null, an empty string, an empty array, false and the string 0, because Number gives 0.0 for every one of them and Number.isFinite is true.", "null and undefined alike, because the filter requires a finite time, a finite ratio and a finite derivative before a sample is kept for the window.", "None of them, because the flat test is applied only after the derivative fit has already succeeded and reported a slope near zero over the whole of the late window."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Only Number(undefined) and Number of the string n/a give NaN, and those are the two spellings that fail the test rather than pass it.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-a-missing-column-read-as-zero', 2, 'The same teaching history, ELELENWO-4 with its derivative column removed, is run twice: once with the column spelled null and once spelled undefined. What separates the two answers?', '["The window, since only the undefined run reaches a late window and the null run is refused before one is opened at all.", "The ratio fit, since the null run returns worSlope 1.040602176 and the undefined run returns no ratio slope because its samples were dropped.", "The ok flag, since the null run returns ok = true on a flat reading and the undefined run returns ok = false with the message about three positive points.", "Spelled null it comes back mechanism displacement at confidence n/a; spelled undefined it comes back mechanism indeterminate at confidence low."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Same missing data, opposite answers, and the reassuring one is the spelling every JSON export and every SQL null produces.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-a-missing-column-read-as-zero', 3, 'What comes back identically from both spellings of the missing derivative column on ELELENWO-4?', '["The ratio fit and the window: worSlope 1.040602176 at worR2 0.921895186 on a late window opening at t = 250.242976 days, with no derivative slope and no span in either run.", "The mechanism, since both runs read the same ratios and therefore reach the same label of displacement on the same evidence.", "The note, since both branches print the sentence about the ratio sitting flat at 9.33 and differ only in the confidence they attach to it.", "The confidence, since a run that fitted no derivative can only come back low whichever way the empty column was spelled."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The ratio evidence is identical in the two runs and only the derivative path differs, which is exactly what makes the divergence a spelling artefact.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-a-missing-column-read-as-zero', 4, 'chanDiagnosis filters its input before it does anything else. What does that filter require of a sample?', '["A finite time, a finite ratio and a finite derivative, which is why a column of nulls is dropped rather than read as flat.", "A finite time, a finite ratio and a ratio above minWor of 0.1, which is the gate that fires on the last sample.", "A finite time and a finite ratio, and nothing at all of the derivative.", "A finite time and a finite derivative, since the derivative is the quantity the mechanism is read off and the ratio is only reported alongside it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The derivative is coerced and tested for magnitude, never for finiteness, and the fails-open follows entirely from that one omission.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-a-missing-column-read-as-zero', 5, 'The teaching gas history is 26 samples exported with the derivative column spelled null. What does chanDiagnosis return, and what does the ratio in it actually do across the window?', '["Mechanism indeterminate with the message about needing three points that are both positive, on a ratio that climbs by a factor of 2.248088575 across the window it was read on.", "Mechanism displacement with a note that nothing is changing, on a gas-oil ratio that runs from 957.197245770 to 2151.864191995 scf/stb, a factor of 2.248088575.", "Mechanism channelling at confidence low, because worSlope 0.356090047 climbs and the flat branch reads the ratio fit before naming a mechanism.", "A refusal, because a gas-oil ratio in scf/stb cannot be read against a water-oil ratio threshold of 0.1 held in a bare ratio."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The flat branch reports that there is nothing on this well for an intervention to fix, on a ratio that rather more than doubled.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-a-missing-column-read-as-zero', 6, 'The flat branch note on the teaching gas history says the ratio is sitting flat at 2151.86. Which field in the same return object contradicts it?', '["derivativeSlope, which comes back at 0.000000000 and is the only quantity the branch actually looked at before printing.", "spanDecades, which comes back n/a and therefore says the window was too short in log cycles for the sentence to have been checked at all.", "worSlope 0.356090047430 at worR2 0.949579197952, a ratio climbing steadily on a line that explains most of it.", "lateFromT of 504.417216 days, which shows the note was written on a window that had already excluded the climbing samples."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The condition is entirely about the derivative and the sentence is entirely about the ratio, and the ratio fit is in the same object saying the opposite.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-a-missing-column-read-as-zero', 7, 'Why did nobody notice that the flat branch asserts something it never checked?', '["The only series it was exercised on really was flat: the published flat history carries every ratio at 1.200000000 and every derivative at 0.000000000, so its worSlope of 0.000000000 agrees with the sentence and even the number printed is right by accident.", "Because the branch is unreachable on any published case, so no test could have run it at all.", "Because the note is suppressed whenever worR2 clears minR2 of 0.5, which the published flat history does not.", "Because the assertion is checked by the oracle against a published expected note on the flat history."]'::jsonb, 0, 'On a series where every sample is 1.200000000 the printed value is every other sample too, so the sentence and the fields never disagreed.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-a-missing-column-read-as-zero', 8, 'Three runs of chanDiagnosis on ELELENWO-4 come back ok = true: the default window, the null derivative column and the undefined derivative column. What is the one run that returns ok = false?', '["The null derivative run, once the flat branch finds no derivative fit to report against minSpanDecades of 0.4.", "A run at a lateFraction outside the range 0.1 to 1.0, which the module rejects as an argument error before it reads a single sample of the history.", "A run whose last sample falls under minWor of 0.1, which short-circuits the entire diagnosis and reports that short circuit through the ok flag on the result.", "A history of fewer than six producing samples, refused with the words that a Chan reading needs a history rather than a handful of points."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Counting samples is the one thing the module does before it reads, and everything it does after reading comes back ok = true.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-a-missing-column-read-as-zero', 9, 'A pipeline gates on if (result.ok) before passing a diagnosis into a screening. What does that check let through?', '["Nothing that matters, because a run with no derivative fit sets ok = false and is caught by exactly that condition.", "A mechanism read off an empty derivative column, since the flat branch is a success path that names displacement with ok = true.", "A refusal on a short history, since ok is true whenever the module produced any note at all, including the one about six producing samples.", "A reading whose derivative slope of 1.442132492 sits inside the ambiguous band of 0.25, which is the borderline case the flag was written to catch."]'::jsonb, 1, 'It is the check that looks like diligence and buys none: it rejects only a history under six samples and accepts every fails-open reading.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-a-missing-column-read-as-zero', 10, 'What should a caller read instead of ok to keep an empty derivative column out of a screening?', '["The three fields that come back n/a together: a named mechanism arriving with no derivativeSlope, no derivativeR2 and no spanDecades reached a verdict without fitting the series the verdict is about.", "worR2, since a ratio fit under minR2 of 0.5 is what an empty column produces.", "The note, since the flat branch prints a sentence no other branch prints.", "confidence alone, since it is n/a on the flat branch and low everywhere else."]'::jsonb, 0, 'On the teaching gas history those three are n/a while worSlope 0.356090047 and worR2 0.949579198 are both fitted and both healthy.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-a-missing-column-read-as-zero', 11, 'skinPiMultiplier returns an object carrying an ok flag. What do pssDenominator, minimumSkin and skinFromPiRatio return for the same bad geometry, and why does that matter at a call site?', '["An object with ok = false and the sentence about the drainage and wellbore radii, which is the same contract in a different shape.", "A zero, which is why a wellbore radius larger than the drainage radius reads through as an undamaged well rather than as a refusal of the geometry.", "A bare NaN, which carries no flag, so an answer cannot be told from a refusal without running Number.isFinite at every call.", "The floor for that geometry, which is the value the successful result also carries and therefore cannot be distinguished from one."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Two failure contracts in one module is what makes the second kind hard to catch, and the bad geometry is a wellbore radius larger than the drainage radius.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-a-missing-column-read-as-zero', 12, 'What does the golden publish about the mechanism the flat branch returns?', '["An expected mechanism of displacement on the flat history, which is why the branch survived review unchanged.", "An expected note for the flat history, which is the sentence the branch prints on every series that reaches it.", "A published late derivative slope for the flat history against which the branch output is asserted.", "Nothing: it publishes four labelled histories, their late derivative slopes, five skin pairs, one geometry floor and one power law, and no expected mechanism, confidence or verdict at all."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The published lateDerivativeSlope for the flat history is null, and chanDiagnosis is asserted against nothing anywhere in the oracle.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-a-missing-column-read-as-zero', 13, 'The flat branch fires when every late sample satisfies Math.abs(p.derivative) < 1e-12. On a history exported without the derivative column, why does every late sample satisfy it?', '["Because the derivative column is absent and an absent property is skipped by the test, which counts a skipped sample as satisfying it.", "Because Number(null) is 0.0 exactly, so the magnitude tested is zero at every sample and the test passes on all of them at once.", "Because the coercion produces NaN and every comparison against NaN is false, which this branch then treats as a pass on its magnitude test.", "Because the samples were dropped by the filter and a branch tested over an empty set of late samples passes by convention."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The branch has no way to tell an empty column from a genuinely flat one, and 26 nulls on the teaching gas history read exactly like 20 zeros on the published flat one.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-a-missing-column-read-as-zero', 14, 'A screening is handed the displacement mechanism that came off a null derivative column. What does it do with it?', '["It refuses to screen the well at all, because a diagnosis arriving with no derivative slope, no fit quality and no span carries nothing for the mechanism gate to act on.", "It marks the water shutoff unknown, which is the verdict reserved for a field the screening could not read.", "It blocks the water shutoff squeeze on the grounds of ordinary displacement, because it reads the mechanism id and nothing about how the mechanism was reached.", "It returns the water shutoff a candidate, because a flat derivative is the signature the squeeze is gated on."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The block reason says the water is arriving because the reservoir is swept, a claim assembled from a column nobody computed.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-a-missing-column-read-as-zero', 15, 'What does the input filter in chanDiagnosis refuse, and what does no field of the result record?', '["It refuses a sample whose time or ratio is not finite, and no field records how many derivatives were coerced from something that was never a number.", "It refuses a sample whose derivative is not finite, and no field records the ratio it was paired with.", "It refuses a whole history whose derivative column is missing at every sample, and no field of the result records which spelling of no value was used.", "It refuses nothing at all, and the count of coerced derivatives is carried out in the note."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The count of samples surviving the filter is visible only through n, which is why a run on 26 coerced nulls looks like an ordinary reading.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 1, 'On the teaching geometry of ELELENWO-4, re 1180 ft and rw 0.354 ft, where does skinPiMultiplier place its only refusal?', '["At the deepest skin the refusal text calls real, which is -6 on a fracture, so an after-skin below that returns ok = false with the sentence about asking for less.", "At the skin where the group reaches zero, which is -7.361728083308, and everything short of that pole is accepted.", "At an after-skin of -5, the deepest acid job the module recognises, which is why the multiplier at -5.000 is the last one returned without a warning attached.", "At the point where the multiplier exceeds the flow efficiency of the undamaged well, which on this geometry is a denominator after of 0.495348054."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The guard is a division-by-zero check, and ln(re/rw) of 8.111728083308 less three quarters is what sets the floor at -7.361728083308.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 2, 'The deepest after-skin this teaching geometry will accept is -7.361, where the denominator after is 0.000728083. What does the call return there?', '["ok = false, with the sentence about the productivity index going infinite, since 0.000728083 is inside the tolerance the pole is tested at.", "ok = true with a multiplier of 20412.125808300 and a warning naming the distance of 0.000728083 remaining above the floor.", "ok = true with the multiplier clamped to the value at -6.800, which is 26.457156986, because the module caps what it will hand back.", "ok = true with a multiplier of 20412.125808300, no warning and no note."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The only thing the guard has established at -7.361 is that the arithmetic did not divide by zero, and a multiplier of 20412.125808300 comes back on that footing.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 3, 'The refusal text at an after-skin of -7.400 names a limit. What is the relationship between the limit it names and the limit it enforces?', '["They are the same limit stated twice, once as a range and once as the floor the geometry allows.", "It names the pole and enforces the fracture limit, so a design at -6.5 is refused before the arithmetic is attempted.", "It names real treatments reaching about -3 to -5 on acid and -5 to -6 on a fracture, and enforces nothing there: the guard sits at the pole of -7.361728083308 instead.", "It names the range only when the geometry is too tight to reach it, so on a wide drainage radius the sentence is suppressed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Everything between -6 and the pole is accepted in silence, with no warning, no note and no flag anywhere on the result.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 4, 'Price the gap between the limit the refusal names and the limit it enforces on the teaching sweep. What is an overreach to an after-skin of -6.800 worth against an honest one at -5.000?', '["A multiplier of 26.457156986 against 6.292734624, a factor of 4.204397383 handed back with the same confidence as the honest number.", "A multiplier of 10.913873530 against 6.292734624, since -6 is where the module''s own refusal text stops calling a fracture treatment real.", "Nothing, because both rows carry the same denominator after and differ only in the flow efficiency they report.", "A factor of 9.189015534, which is the whole distance between the pole at -7.361728083308 and the deepest acid job the refusal text describes."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The factor of 9.189015534 is the same overreach measured against the designed acid job, 7.5 down to -2.2, which returns 2.879215612184.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 5, 'The floor a geometry allows sits inside every successful multiplier result. Against what does the module ever test that value?', '["The after-skin requested, which is how the pole guard is implemented and the reason a request at -7.400 comes back refused.", "Nothing but zero, so the one number in the object that could have flagged an overreach sits there unused.", "The range the refusal text names, which is why a design at -6.800 returns without a note.", "The before-skin, which is the comparison that produces the sentence about the equation running out rather than a well."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The floor of -7.361728083308 is in the result of every successful call and the module never asks whether the design got anywhere near it.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 6, 'A vendor claims an 8.500 fold uplift on the teaching geometry. What does skinFromPiRatio return and what does it say about the claim?', '["A refusal, because -6.495642426 is past the fracture limit of -6 the module''s own text names.", "An implied skin of -6.495642426 together with the floor of -7.361728083 for this geometry, so that a caller has both halves of the comparison in one place.", "A bare NaN, because a claimed uplift implying a skin below the fracture limit is treated as bad input.", "An implied post-job skin of -6.495642426, and nothing at all: there is no ok flag, no warning list and no plausibility check on that function."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It returns a bare number on both sides of the crossing between plausible and not, and the audit is a comparison the caller has to write.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 7, 'On the published geometry, floor -7.900724584041, a claimed uplift of 15.000 times implies a skin of -7.374009612. How far above the floor is that, and how is it reported?', '["0.526714972 above the floor, and reported through the same ok = false contract skinPiMultiplier uses at the pole.", "0.395036229 above the floor, which is the distance that would be flagged if the claim were checked against the range the module calls achievable.", "0.526714972 above the floor, returned with no flag, no warning and no note.", "It is below the floor, so the function returns a bare NaN and the claim is refused by arithmetic even though nothing checks plausibility."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Claims of 20.000 and 30.000 times come back the same way at 0.395036229 and 0.263357486 above the floor, all past everything the module calls achievable.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 8, 'The zero-variance guard in logLogSlope is written as r2 = syy > 0 ? (sxy * sxy) / (sxx * syy) : 1. Where does the branch after the colon actually run?', '["On four points with y identical at 5, where it returns exactly the r2 of 1.000000e+0 it exists to produce, 0.000000e+0 short of the intended value.", "Nowhere: the branch is dead code, because syy over identical y never accumulates to exactly zero in floating point.", "On any window whose derivative is constant, which is the case it was written for and the case it catches.", "On the twenty sample constant-derivative demonstration, whose derivative is 0.900000000 at every sample, which is why that series comes back at a perfect fit quality."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Four copies of one logarithm average back to that logarithm exactly, so every deviation is zero and syy > 0 is false.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 9, 'The derived sweep of the zero-variance guard runs the sample count against the value. What does it establish?', '["That it fires below n = 8 and never above, which is a rule a caller can hold in their head and check against.", "That neither the count nor the value predicts it alone: it fires at n = 3 and n = 4 on every value tried, splits at n = 5 where y = 5 fires and y = 0.9 does not, and y = 2 keeps firing until n = 32.", "That it fires on small values and not on large ones, so a ratio in scf/stb never reaches it and a bare water-oil ratio always does.", "That it never fires on real data because the sums are accumulated in a different order from the sweep."]'::jsonb, 1, 'There is no rule here: whether syy reaches exactly zero over identical y depends on the sample count and the value together.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 10, 'Why is a guard that fires on small tidy cases and stops firing on real ones worse than one that never fires at all?', '["Because a guard that never fires is removed by the compiler, while this one costs a comparison at every call.", "Because it fires on the published cases and not on teaching cases, so the golden covers it and the field does not.", "Dead code is harmless once you know it is dead, and this pattern passes a test suite on the four point case and then fails in production on the twenty sample one.", "Because it returns a fit quality of 1.000000e+0 where the ordinary formula would have returned a higher one."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The derived four point case at y = 5 fires and returns the intended 1.000000e+0, and the twenty sample history comes back at 2.271680e-31.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 11, 'The constant-derivative demonstration is WOR = 2.0 + 0.9 ln t over 20 samples from t = 20 to 2000 days, with the derivative at 0.900000000 at every sample. What does chanDiagnosis make of it?', '["Mechanism displacement, because a derivative that never changes is exactly the flat branch condition and 0.900000000 sits within 1e-12 of itself at every one of the samples.", "Mechanism coning, because the derivative fit returns a slope of -9.465333e-33 and any negative slope clears coningSlope of -0.1.", "A refusal with ok = false, because a series whose derivative carries no variance cannot be fitted and the module counts before it reads.", "Mechanism indeterminate at confidence low, on a derivative fit quality of 0.000000000, refused with the note that the fit explains only 0.0 percent of it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The derivative fit on its own returns an r-squared of 2.271680e-31 against a minR2 of 0.5, on a series with no scatter in it at all.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 12, 'The demonstration''s refusal says the fit explains only 0.0 percent of it while minR2 is 0.5. What is going on with those two numbers?', '["They are one fit quality in two scales: the engine holds it as a fraction in the defaults and prints it as a percentage in the error message.", "They are two different quantities, since minR2 of 0.5 gates the ratio fit while the percentage in the message describes the derivative fit alone.", "The message is rounding a fit quality of 2.271680e-31 up to 0.0 percent, which is why it then fails a gate written at 0.5 percent.", "The 0.5 is a slope threshold on the derivative and the percentage is a fit quality, so the two never meet."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s own numbers are a fraction of 2.271680e-31 and a message reading 0.0 percent, and saying which scale is meant is required every time.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 13, 'The refusal on that demonstration is not wrong about its own test. What is it wrong about?', '["The threshold, since a fit quality of 2.271680e-31 would have cleared a minR2 of 0.5 held as a percentage rather than as a fraction.", "The window, since the late window opening at 225.767578 days holds fewer samples than the whole series of 20, and a wider window over the same data would have passed the gate.", "The well: it says the derivative scatters too much to carry a slope on a series whose derivative is 0.900000000 at every sample and has no scatter whatever.", "The mechanism, since a constant derivative is the displacement signature and indeterminate is the wrong label for it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A fit quality measures how much of the spread in y a line accounts for, so a series with no spread scores zero on a test it was never a candidate for.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 14, 'The low-last-sample demonstration climbs to 20.279117393 at its second to last reading and ends with a post-shut-in test of 0.060000000. What does minWor of 0.1 do with that history?', '["It drops the low sample and reads the remaining 23, which is why the slope of 1.150000000 still comes back.", "It short-circuits the whole diagnosis on that one final reading, returning mechanism displacement with every slope, every fit quality, the span and the window start unavailable.", "It lowers the confidence to low and flags the reading ambiguous, which is what the restored run returns.", "It refuses with ok = false, because a ratio under the threshold is the one input the module counts before it reads."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Restore that single sample and the same call returns worSlope 1.150000000 at 1.000000000 over a span of 0.872304180 log cycles from 268.364963 days.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 15, 'minWor is 0.1 and is documented for a water-oil ratio. What happens when the column handed in is a gas-oil ratio in scf/stb?', '["The gate is applied to the mean of the series instead, because a gas-oil ratio has no single last reading the module trusts.", "The gate refuses the history, because a threshold named for one fluid cannot be compared against a column carrying another.", "The gate scales itself by the expected gas-oil ratio of 950 scf/stb, which is the comparison the gas screening already makes.", "The gate is inert: the last gas-oil ratio of 2151.864192 scf/stb clears a threshold of 0.1 by a factor of 2.151864e+4."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The threshold is a bare 0.1 applied unchanged to whatever arrived, so it is four orders of magnitude away from doing anything on gas.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-screening-cannot-know', 1, 'The gas shutoff gate is a factor of two on the expected gas-oil ratio and nothing else. Where does the derived sweep put the boundary against an expected 950 scf/stb?', '["Between 1500 scf/stb and 1900 scf/stb, since the verdict moves once the ratio to expected clears 1.578947368 and the gas becomes worth screening.", "Between 1899 scf/stb, which returns no at a ratio to expected of 1.998947368, and 1900 scf/stb, which returns consider at exactly 2.000000000.", "At 2152 scf/stb, the teaching well''s own ratio, which returns consider at a ratio to expected of 2.265263158 and is the lowest ratio in the sweep to do so.", "At 3000 scf/stb, a ratio to expected of 3.157894737, because the gate wants the gas to be three times the expectation before it is worth a squeeze."]'::jsonb, 1, 'One scf/stb of gas-oil ratio separates a verdict of no with one reason from a verdict of consider with two, and 2.000000000 is the whole test.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-screening-cannot-know', 2, 'The second reason on the gas shutoff verdict ends by telling the user to run the diagnostic on the gas-oil ratio. What happens when a planner does that and passes the whole result object onward?', '["The water shutoff comes back blocked on gas evidence, with reason 1 quoting a water cut of 75 percent and a block reason about the reservoir being swept.", "The screening refuses to act on it, because a diagnosis carrying a gas-oil ratio in scf/stb cannot be gated against a water cut held in percent.", "The gas shutoff verdict is upgraded from consider to candidate, since the diagnosis now supplies the mechanism that particular squeeze is gated on and nothing else was missing.", "The water shutoff is left unknown, which is the verdict the screening returns for a field it could not read on this well."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Not one word of that block was read off water, and the well row handed in is identical to the one that returns a candidate on the water diagnosis.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-screening-cannot-know', 3, 'What field on the diagnosis would let screenTreatments tell a water reading from a gas reading?', '["lateFromT, since a gas history and a water history open their late windows at different times on this well, 504.417216 days against 250.242976 days, which the screening compares.", "worSlope, since a gas-oil ratio slope of 0.356090047 is an order of magnitude flatter than a water-oil ratio slope of 1.040602176.", "There is none: nothing chanDiagnosis returns names the fluid, and the screening reads diagnosis.mechanism.id and nothing else about how the reading was reached.", "The note, since the sentence printed on a gas run names the ratio in scf/stb and the sentence printed on a water run does not."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The screening cannot refuse what it cannot see, and a mechanism id of displacement carries no record of the column it came from.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-screening-cannot-know', 4, 'screenTreatments reads diagnosis.confidence in exactly one branch. Which one, and what does that placement mean?', '["The coning branch, which is the one qualitatively different reading in the classifier and therefore the one the module attaches its warning to.", "The branch that fires when no diagnosis was handed in at all, which is the honest refusal about the mechanism not having been established.", "Every branch that blocks, since a block is the verdict that costs a well its oil and therefore the one the caveat belongs on.", "The channelling branch, so the caveat is attached to the verdict that spends money and not to the ones that refuse to."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At lateFraction 0.5 the water shutoff carries three reasons including the line about confirming with a production log, and at 0.9 it blocks with one reason.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-screening-cannot-know', 5, 'The same 38 samples read at lateFraction 0.5 and at 0.9 both return confidence low and ambiguous true. What does the screening do with those two fields on each run?', '["It reads them on both, which is why both verdicts come back with the same reason count of 3.", "It reads them on the channelling run at 0.5 and appends a sentence, and reads neither on the displacement run at 0.9, which blocks without mentioning either.", "It reads neither of them on the channelling run and reads both on the displacement run, on the principle that a block is the verdict which has to justify itself in writing.", "It reads ambiguous on both and confidence on neither, which is why the flag appears in the reason list on each side."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A block on a reading that is low confidence and ambiguous is written exactly as a block on a reading at confidence high, and only the candidate says how sure it is.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-screening-cannot-know', 6, 'The hydraulic fracture verdict is a ternary testing whether the skin is finite and above zero. What does the derived sweep across skin show it deciding?', '["That it moves from consider to candidate above a skin of 2, in step with matrix acid on the same field, which is what the ternary reads as though it were written to do.", "That it moves to unknown when no skin was entered at all, which is the one row where the test has any visible effect on the verdict.", "Nothing: consider comes back at every skin from -4.000 to 20.000 and again when no skin was entered, with three reasons on every row.", "That it moves from consider down to no below a skin of zero, on the grounds that a fracture is not a damage treatment and pays nothing on an undamaged well."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both arms of the ternary return the same string, so the skin test evaluates and its result is discarded, and the reason count stays at 3 throughout.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-screening-cannot-know', 7, 'The row where no skin was entered is the one that proves the fracture ternary decides nothing. Why is that row the proof?', '["Because the test takes its other arm there, the one reached when the skin is not finite, and the answer is still the same word while matrix acid on the same field returns unknown.", "Because a missing skin makes the whole screening return unknown, so the fracture verdict of consider is the only one out of step.", "Because the reason count drops on that row, which is the effect the skin test was meant to produce.", "Because the verdict order puts unknown below consider, so a missing skin should have moved the fracture down the ranking."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Matrix acid is gated on the same field and returns unknown when no skin was entered, so the module knows how to write that branch.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-screening-cannot-know', 8, 'The module writes three skin-gated or well-gated verdicts correctly. What are their rules?', '["Matrix acid is candidate above a skin of 8, recompletion above 2, and artificial lift above 30 percent water.", "All three are gated on the mechanism id alone, which is why they move together when the window fraction changes.", "Matrix acid and recompletion are gated on the skin and artificial lift on the confidence, which is the field the channelling branch reads.", "Matrix acid is candidate above a skin of 2, marginal above 0, no at or below 0 and unknown with no skin entered; recompletion is candidate when the mechanism is channelling or the skin is above 8; artificial lift is candidate when flowing is false and consider above 70 percent water."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Three working gates in one function is why the fracture defect survived review: it has the same shape and the same call signature as its neighbours.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-screening-cannot-know', 9, 'What does the oracle check to machine precision, and by what independent routes?', '["The mechanism against a published expected label on each of the four labelled histories, and the multiplier against a published value on each of the five published skin pairs.", "The log-log slope by Theil-Sen, the median of every pairwise slope, and the skin uplift by a full radial Darcy rate built in SI with permeability in square metres.", "The late derivative slope on each published history and the block reason the screening returns for it.", "The fit quality on the power law and the confidence the classifier assigns to each published history."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On the published power law the engine reproduces a slope of 1.350000000000 with a difference of 0.0000e+0, and the largest disagreement on the five skin pairs is -8.8818e-16.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-screening-cannot-know', 10, 'Which functions in this module are asserted against nothing at all?', '["Only skinFromPiRatio, since the round trip through skinPiMultiplier is checked and returns the stated skin to a difference of 8.8818e-16.", "None of them: every exported function is covered, because the golden publishes four labelled histories and five skin pairs.", "chanDiagnosis, screenTreatments, rankTreatments and skinFromPiRatio, which between them are everything that returns a verdict.", "logLogSlope and pssDenominator, since the golden publishes only a power law and a geometry floor and never exercises them on a real history."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The golden publishes no expected mechanism, no expected confidence, no expected verdict, no expected refusal and no expected block reason.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-screening-cannot-know', 11, 'On the published coning history the two estimators separate. By how much, and what does the separation say?', '["The published Theil-Sen slope is -0.555098339661 and the engine returns -0.539955222223, a difference of 1.514312e-2, on data with no noise in it, because the shape is not a power law and the two estimators weight a curved log-log trend differently.", "The difference is 2.220446e-16, which is machine precision, so the two agree and the separation is only apparent.", "The engine slope fails minR2 of 0.5 at a fit quality of 0.948751314445, so the disagreement is a refusal rather than a separation.", "The difference is a relative -2.728006e-2 caused by noise in the published history, which is why the golden publishes a tolerance for it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both routes are right about their own question and neither is a measurement of a slope that exists, at a fit quality of 0.948751314445 as a fraction.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-screening-cannot-know', 12, 'The published displacement history comes back at confidence high with ambiguous false on a derivative slope of exactly 1.000000000000. Why is that the reading worth arguing with?', '["Because the published lateDerivativeSlope for that particular history comes back as null, so the verdict rests entirely on a quantity the golden itself declined to publish at all.", "Because 1.000000000000 sits inside the ambiguous band and the flag should have come back true rather than false.", "Because the engine and the Theil-Sen route disagree by 4.440892e-16 on that history, which is the largest gap in the golden.", "Because a slope of one is the case the oracle''s own docstring calls the one that genuinely needs the plot and a person, and the classifier is at its most certain there."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A slope of 1.000000000000 at a fit quality of 1.000000000000 is exactly the arithmetic being right and the sentence beside it being unchecked.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-screening-cannot-know', 13, 'Hand screenTreatments no diagnosis at all on the teaching well. What comes back on the water shutoff, and why does it read differently from the other blocks?', '["Unknown, since the mechanism field is the one the screening reads and it was not supplied.", "Blocked, with the reason that the water cut is 75 percent and worth acting on but not until it is known what is bringing the water, and a block reason that names its own uncertainty.", "A candidate, since the water cut of 75 percent clears the gate at 30 percent and no mechanism has yet refused it.", "A refusal with ok = false, which is the contract the module uses whenever a required input is missing."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That block says a shutoff squeeze on a coning well is money down a hole and this history does not say which it is, which is the shape the other blocks are missing.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-screening-cannot-know', 14, 'Two screening runs on the teaching well produce different rankings. What is the ordering vocabulary they are ranked in?', '["candidate, consider, marginal, unknown, blocked, no, which is why a blocked verdict outranks one that was never read.", "no, blocked, marginal, consider, candidate, unknown, since the ranking is built from the least promising treatment upward.", "candidate, consider, marginal, blocked, unknown, no.", "candidate, marginal, consider, no, blocked, unknown, which puts the two refusals at the bottom of every list a planner reads."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At lateFraction 0.5 the order runs matrix acid, water shutoff and recompletion as candidates, and at 0.9 the water shutoff falls to blocked.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-screening-cannot-know', 15, 'A planner reads a block as more certain than a candidate. What is wrong with that reading on this well?', '["Nothing, since a block is issued only on a confidence of high, which is what the published coning and displacement histories both return at the default window.", "The block is issued on a reading whose fit quality of 0.995286721 is under minR2 of 0.5, so it is the weaker of the two.", "The candidate is the more certain of the two, because it carries three reasons against the single reason on the block, and the reason count is how this module records certainty.", "Both readings carry confidence low and ambiguous true, only the candidate says so in print, and a block costs money too in the oil the well would have made."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The verdict at 0.9 blocks with one reason on a reading at confidence low, and the branch that issues it never opens the field.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 1, 'The working order puts one check ahead of every fit. Which is it, and what is the reasoning behind putting it first?', '["Sweep the window fraction, because the analyst''s choice moves the derivative slope by 0.370920348 and everything downstream inherits that choice.", "Look at the derivative column and its spelling, because a column of nulls reaches the flat branch and every number computed after that point is correctly computed from zeros.", "Fit the whole history with no window, because the raw fit is the only reading in the module that no classifier has touched.", "Compare the after-skin against the range of about -3 to -5 on acid and -5 to -6 on a fracture, because that is the only plausibility test in the module."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A run on a coerced column returns worSlope 1.040602176 and a named mechanism, and every one of those numbers is arithmetically right.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 2, 'Working out of order is what makes a screening indefensible. Which step, taken late, leaves every later number correct and the conclusion wrong?', '["Ranking the treatments, since the ranked order is the part of the output a planner reads first and it moves whenever the mechanism moves under it.", "Reading minimumSkin, since it is computed on every call to the multiplier and handed back inside the successful result where a caller can miss it.", "Counting the samples, since a history of fewer than six producing points is refused outright and that refusal costs nothing at all to obtain first.", "Establishing what the verdict was read on, since a slope, a fit quality and a span can each be exactly right about a window nobody wrote down."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A margin of 0.142132492 is a property of the window opening at t = 250.242976 days and not a property of the well.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 3, 'This engine sizes nothing. What is the only dimensional number it returns anywhere?', '["The derivative slope, which carries units of ratio per log cycle and is the quantity the mechanism is read from.", "The span in log cycles, which is the one measured length in the whole return object and the one the span gate tests.", "The start of the late window in days.", "The multiplier from skinPiMultiplier, which is a productivity index ratio and therefore carries the units of a rate."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every verdict it returns is a string or a boolean, a log-log slope carries no unit at all, and a fit quality is a bare fraction.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 4, 'What follows from the fact that the module returns no rate, no volume, no time to payout and no cost?', '["A treatment it calls a candidate is a candidate on the evidence and not yet on the money, so the economics are a separate piece of work entirely.", "The ranking is unusable, because candidate, consider, marginal, blocked, unknown and no cannot be ordered without a cost.", "The screening compensates by weighting the reasons it prints, which is why the water shutoff carries three of them at the default window and one when it blocks.", "The verdicts are equivalent to an economic screen, because the module gates on water cut, gas-oil ratio and skin, which are the three drivers of value."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It ranks what to do about the answer in the order candidate, consider, marginal, blocked, unknown, no, and prices none of it.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 5, 'The tier ends on four questions to ask of any returned diagnosis. What are they?', '["Which mechanism, which confidence, which treatable flag and which ranked order.", "Which window it was read on, which samples survived the filter, which column was actually read, and which assertion would have caught it wrong.", "Which threshold decided it, which fit quality supports it, which span in log cycles the line sat on, and which of the module''s own notes were printed beside it.", "Which fluid it read, which water cut the well is carrying, which skin was entered against it, and which drainage radius and wellbore radius the geometry was built on."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fourth has no answer in this module, because chanDiagnosis and screenTreatments are asserted against nothing at all.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 6, 'The checklist has a row for the two fits inside one return object. What does passing that row look like?', '["The two slopes agree to within the ambiguous band of 0.25, which is the test that says the window was well chosen and that a mechanism can be read off the pair at all.", "The two fit qualities are both above minR2 of 0.5, held as a fraction, which is what licenses reading a mechanism off either one of them.", "The point counts and the spans are kept separate, because the ratio slope was measured over every late sample and the derivative slope over only the positive ones.", "The two spans are added together to recover the length of plot the reading rested on."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At the default window on the teaching well that is 19 samples against 15, and a gap of 0.401530316 between two slopes reported side by side.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 7, 'The checklist wants a span above 0.4 and a fit quality above 0.5. What are those two numbers?', '["The ambiguous band and the coning threshold, which bracket the region where a verdict is provisional.", "The clamp floor on lateFraction and the water gate below which no intervention is worth screening.", "The minimum window length and the minimum fraction of positive samples a derivative fit will accept.", "minSpanDecades and minR2, both named defaults, the first in log cycles and the second a fraction."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine holds the fit quality as a fraction and prints it as a percentage in its own error text, so 0.5 and 0.0 percent are one quantity in two scales.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 8, 'Why does the working order insist on Number.isFinite at the skin call sites?', '["Because skinPiMultiplier reports failure through an ok flag while pssDenominator, minimumSkin and skinFromPiRatio return a bare NaN, which cannot be told from an answer.", "Because a skin below the floor is returned as a negative multiplier rather than as a refusal.", "Because the multiplier is exactly 1 when the skin does not change, and a caller cannot tell that from a failure.", "Because a bare NaN is printed inside the refusal text rather than returned on the object, so the value has to be recovered by parsing that message at every one of the call sites."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two failure contracts in one module is what the check exists for, and the geometry that triggers both is a wellbore radius larger than the drainage radius.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 9, 'A well carries a rate cut followed by a falling ratio, which is the coning field test, and is still reported as channelling at a derivative fit quality of 0.998513658. How?', '["The coning branch was reached and then overridden, because 1.442132492 clears channellingSlope of 1.3 and the steeper reading wins.", "The falling samples were fitted and the magnitude slope of -0.850000000000 was averaged in with the rising slope, which lands the combined result above the channelling threshold.", "The falling samples were removed before the derivative fit, so the reading was taken on the stretch that was still climbing and the fit quality grades that stretch alone.", "The reading came back ambiguous, and an ambiguous reading defaults to channelling so that a treatable mechanism is not missed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The stretch the engine discarded fits at 0.999955540052 as a fraction, cleaner than the 0.998513658 it reported for the verdict it gave.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 10, 'Coning is described as the one qualitatively different reading in this module. What makes it different, and what is its answer?', '["It is the only mechanism the golden publishes an expected label for, and its answer is a shutoff squeeze placed across the bottom perforations of the existing completion.", "Its derivative falls rather than climbs, below a coningSlope of -0.1, and its answer is not a treatment at all: less drawdown, a different completion, or living with it.", "It is the only mechanism separated from the others by a threshold rather than by a shape, and its answer is a recompletion.", "It is the only reading that comes back treatable, and its answer is the water shutoff squeeze the screening ranks first."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Channelling and displacement are separated by steepness alone across a boundary at 1.3, which is why coning is the firm end of the reading.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 11, 'Why is a boundary at 1.3 with a band of 0.25 called a convention rather than a measurement?', '["Because the thresholds are overridable, and any value a caller supplies is accepted without a check.", "Because the engine measures the slope by ordinary least squares while the oracle measures it by Theil-Sen, so the boundary lands in a different place on each of the two routes.", "Because the band is applied to the ratio slope and the threshold to the derivative slope, so the two are not on the same scale.", "Because for any power-law history the ratio and its derivative have the same log-log slope, so nothing separates displacement from channelling except how steep the climb is."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The module''s own comment concedes it, which is why the threshold and not the physics decides whether a squeeze is recommended.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 12, 'Before any window is chosen, the order says to fit the raw history and set the point count against the rows handed in. Why that comparison?', '["Because the fit silently drops what it cannot take a logarithm of, so a returned n below the count supplied is the only record that anything was removed.", "Because the raw fit is the one reading the golden actually asserts, so its point count is the single number an independent route would ever have caught wrong here.", "Because the intercept is only meaningful when every row handed in was actually used, and the fit goes on reporting an intercept whether or not that happens to be so.", "Because a point count above the rows handed in means the window was clamped and the history read twice."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The full-history ratio fit on the teaching well returns n = 38 having dropped 0 of the 38 handed to it, and it says nothing about that either way.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 13, 'What does the module do genuinely well, and how is that established?', '["Its classifier is checked against four labelled histories with expected mechanisms, which is the strongest assertion in the golden.", "Its screening is checked against a published block reason for each of the seven treatments.", "Its two pure arithmetic functions are checked by independent routes to machine precision: a slope of 1.350000000000 reproduced with a difference of 0.0000e+0, and a published multiplier of 2.012565355861 matched the same way.", "Its refusals are checked, because the golden publishes the exact wording of each error the module can return."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Theil-Sen and a radial Darcy rate built in SI are two routes that share nothing with the engine''s own arithmetic, and both hold.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 14, 'The closing limit of the tier is that a number in this module can be exactly right and the sentence beside it still wrong. What supports that claim?', '["The slopes are computed in one scale and printed in another, so a correct number is reported wrongly in the note.", "The arithmetic functions are asserted to machine precision and every function that returns a verdict is asserted against nothing, so the sentence was never checked by anyone.", "The notes are assembled from the fields, so a wrong sentence implies a wrong field somewhere in the same object.", "The verdicts are ranked before they are written, so the ordering can be right while the individual verdict is wrong."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The flat branch is the cleanest case: it prints that the ratio is sitting flat while worSlope 0.356090047 in the same object says it climbed.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 15, 'Units are part of the reading here. Which set does the module work in?', '["Days, ft, scf/stb throughout, percent for both cuts and ratios, and log cycles for slopes as well as spans.", "SI throughout, since the oracle builds its check with permeability in square metres and pressures in pascals.", "Days, ft, a bare ratio for both fluids, and a percentage for every fit quality, which is the scale the defaults are held in.", "Days for producing time, ft for radii, a bare ratio for water-oil, scf/stb for gas-oil, percent for water cut, dimensionless for skin and for every log-log slope, and log cycles for spans."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The oracle works in SI to build its independent route, and the engine and this course do not, so a log-log slope carries no unit at all.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 1, 'A report quotes the headline derivative slope range of 0.370920348 on the teaching well as the measure of how much the analyst''s choice is worth. What has to be said alongside it?', '["That its bottom end of 1.229355999 is the full-history fit and therefore the only reading with no window in it, which makes the range an artefact of comparing two different functions.", "That its top end of 1.600276347 was taken off a window the engine declined to read, returning mechanism indeterminate with no span key while leaving the slope and its fit quality fully populated.", "That the range is measured against channellingSlope of 1.3 and so cannot be compared with the ambiguous band of 0.25, which is a width on the same quantity.", "That the range collapses once the four falling samples are added back, because they flatten every window equally."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A refused reading still hands back its slope, so the steepest number anywhere in the sweep is the one the module said it could not read.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 2, 'Dropping the falling samples on the teaching well costs 0.257320134 of a log cycle. Which guard is positioned to notice that loss, and what does it actually test?', '["minR2 at 0.5, which tests the fit quality of the surviving line and rises as samples are removed, so the loss makes the gate easier to pass, which is why a shortened window scores higher.", "The ambiguous band of 0.25, which widens as the fitted span shortens and is the module''s compensation for a shortened window, and it widens by the size of the loss.", "minSpanDecades at 0.4, which tests only the span that survived the filter, so it fires on the consequence of the drop and never names the drop itself.", "None: there is no span gate at all, and the reported span is descriptive rather than tested."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At lateFraction 0.30 the reported span of 0.450310235 clears the gate by 0.050310235 after a loss of 0.257320134, so the gate is decided by the filter.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 3, 'A planner follows the module''s own instruction to run the diagnostic on the gas-oil ratio, using an export whose Bourdet derivative column was never computed, and passes the result into the screening. How many separate behaviours have to line up for the water shutoff to come back blocked?', '["Three: null coerces to 0.0 and passes the flat test, the flat branch names displacement on that basis, and the screening reads only the mechanism id so it blocks the water job on gas evidence.", "Two: the gas-oil ratio of 2152 scf/stb clears the gate at twice the expected 950, and the screening then ranks the gas shutoff above the water shutoff, so the water job is never reached on this run.", "One: the screening reads the fluid from the diagnosis and finds a gas history where it expected water.", "Four: the ratio gate at 0.1 fires, the span gate fires, the fit quality gate fires and the mechanism defaults to displacement."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The block reason quotes a water cut of 75 percent and a swept reservoir, assembled from a column nobody computed.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 4, 'Two runs of one well at two window fractions both come back at confidence low with ambiguous true, one as a candidate and one as a block. Which run carries the caveat in print, and why?', '["The block, because a refusal has to justify itself and the displacement branch appends the confidence to its block reason, which is where the caveat is printed in full.", "Both, because the ambiguous flag is copied into the reason list of every treatment the screening returns, whether that treatment is ranked or blocked.", "Neither, because confidence is a field of the diagnosis and the screening reads only the ranked verdicts, and never the diagnosis object they came from.", "The candidate, because confidence is read inside the channelling branch and nowhere else, so the caveat lands on the verdict that spends money."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At lateFraction 0.5 the water shutoff carries 3 reasons and at 0.9 it blocks with 1, on readings that are equally uncertain.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 5, 'Rate reduction comes back no on the teaching well with the reason that the diagnostic does not say coning. What in the same run had already argued for coning?', '["The ratio slope of 1.040602176, which is flatter than the derivative slope and therefore the coning signature, which is a falling reading by definition.", "The 4 late samples with a falling derivative, which the fit removed and which the engine counted two lines later and then discarded.", "The ambiguous flag, which comes back true and is the module''s way of recording a second mechanism in contention, set against the one it printed.", "The fit quality of 0.998513658, which is high enough that only a coning history could produce it, on a late window with no scatter left in it at all."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A rate cut followed by a falling ratio is the coning field test, and the count of 4 is read on no branch this run reaches.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 6, 'Two teaching series each have a derivative with no variance in it whatever. Why do they come back with opposite answers?', '["One is read on a late window and the other on the whole history, so the two are not comparable readings at all.", "One has 26 samples and the other 20, and the flat branch requires more than 24 late samples before it will fire.", "One is an empty column coerced to exactly 0.0, which passes the flat test and returns displacement; the other is a genuine constant of 0.900000000, which is fitted and returns a fit quality of 2.271680e-31 under a minR2 of 0.5.", "One carries a ratio that climbs and the other a ratio that is flat, and the branch is selected on the ratio fit."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The reassuring answer comes from the missing data and the refusal comes from the real signature, which is the wrong way round.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 7, 'Name the two places in this module where a caller cannot tell a decision from the absence of one.', '["skinFromPiRatio, which returns a bare NaN with no ok flag for bad geometry, and the hydraulic fracture ternary, which returns consider whether or not the skin test passes.", "The ambiguous flag, which is n/a on some branches, and the treatable flag, which is false on three of the four mechanisms, so neither of those fields carries a decision of its own.", "The ranked order, which puts unknown between blocked and no, and the reason count, which is 3 on both sides of the fracture branch, so the count cannot separate them either.", "lateFromT, which is n/a when the ratio gate fires, and spanDecades, which is n/a when the span gate fires."]'::jsonb, 0, 'One hands back a number that is not an answer and the other hands back an answer that is not a decision, and neither carries a flag.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 8, 'Two different silent inputs on one history produce a mechanism the analyst never asked for. What are they, and what does each return?', '["A lateFraction above 1.00, clamped to the ceiling and returning coning, and a ratio column below minWor of 0.1, returning indeterminate, with no slope and no confidence computed anywhere.", "A history of fewer than six samples, refused with ok = false, and a derivative column spelled undefined, returning displacement.", "A negative lateFraction, refused as an argument error, and an empty derivative column, refused as missing data.", "A lateFraction below 0.10, clamped to the floor and returning coning off the four falling samples, and a derivative column spelled null, returning displacement off the flat branch."]'::jsonb, 3, 'One is the dial clamped to 0.1 and the other is Number(null) reading as 0.0, and neither leaves any record on the result.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 9, 'Of the four questions this tier asks of any returned diagnosis, which one cannot be answered from inside the module?', '["Which window it was read on, because the return object carries no field naming the fraction that produced it, only the start of that window in days.", "Which assertion would have caught it wrong, because the golden publishes no expected mechanism, confidence, verdict, refusal or block reason.", "Which samples survived, because the returned point count is taken after both filters have run, so the count that was handed in is lost entirely.", "Which column was read, because nothing on the diagnosis names the fluid the ratio came from, and the screening then reads the mechanism id alone."]'::jsonb, 1, 'chanDiagnosis, screenTreatments, rankTreatments and skinFromPiRatio are asserted against nothing at all, and the answer is the plot.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 10, 'Why does the working order call for Number.isFinite at every skin call site, and something entirely different at the diagnosis call site?', '["Because the skin functions can return a negative multiplier while the diagnosis can only return a string.", "Because the skin functions are checked by the oracle and the diagnosis is not, so only the unchecked one needs a guard.", "Because the skin functions split across two failure contracts, one with an ok flag and three with a bare NaN, while chanDiagnosis returns ok = true on every reading it manages to produce.", "Because the skin functions accept a geometry and the diagnosis accepts a series, so a finite check is meaningful only on the first, since a series cannot be tested for finiteness in one call."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Only a history of fewer than six producing samples comes back ok = false, so if (result.ok) accepts a mechanism read off an empty column.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 11, 'An audit sets the sweep margin at lateFraction 0.80, which is -0.007367476, against the loss of 0.257320134 that the derivative filter costs at every window. What does the comparison show?', '["That a quantity no instrument could resolve decides the spend, while a quantity thirty times larger is given away silently before the slope is even fitted.", "That the two are on different scales, since a margin is a slope and a loss is a span, so they cannot be compared at all.", "That the filter is conservative, because the loss always moves the slope away from the threshold and therefore towards the safer verdict, which is the block rather than the squeeze.", "That the margin would be positive if the filter were disabled, which is why the drop is the cause of the block at that window, and restoring the samples would clear 1.3."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The block at 0.80 rests on 0.007367476 of slope, and the fit that produced it had already given up more than half of a minSpanDecades gate of 0.4.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 12, 'A history exported with nulls in the derivative column is read at three different window fractions. What changes between the runs?', '["The mechanism, since the clamp floor returns coning and the ceiling returns displacement even on a coerced column, because the clamp is applied well before the flat test is ever reached.", "The confidence, which moves from n/a to low as the window widens and more coerced samples enter the flat test.", "Nothing at all, because the flat branch is reached before the window is opened and lateFraction is never applied.", "The window start and the ratio fit, and nothing else: every run takes the flat branch, names displacement and reports no derivative slope, no derivative fit quality and no span."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The flat test is satisfied at every late sample whatever the window, because Number(null) is 0.0 at all of them.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 13, 'logLogSlope is handed six points of which two are negative and returns ok = true with n = 4. What has the caller lost that no field of the result records?', '["The intercept, which is only reported when every point handed in survives the positivity filter, and here two of the six did not survive it.", "Two of their points and 0.602059991 of a log cycle, since the fit describes 0.903089987 against the 1.505149978 the six points covered.", "The fit quality, which comes back as 1.000000000 on the survivors and is therefore not a statement about the six, only about the four that were fitted.", "Nothing, because the returned n of 4 is the record and the caller supplied the six, so the difference between them is available to be taken."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The n after the drop is visible and the count handed in is not, so the loss has to be tracked outside the function.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 14, 'chanDiagnosis counts the late samples whose derivative is finite and below zero. What has to be true of a history for that count to be read?', '["The derivative fit has to fail, or every late derivative has to sit inside 1e-12 of zero, because those are the only two branches that open it.", "The count has to reach a threshold, and the threshold is the ambiguous band of 0.25 applied to the fraction of samples dropped, which is how the band is reused here.", "The reading has to come back at confidence low, because the count is used to explain a low confidence in the note, alongside the ambiguous flag on that reading.", "The window has to be at the default lateFraction of 0.5, because the count is computed only there, and every other window fraction skips the line entirely."]'::jsonb, 0, 'On a well with 15 rising and 4 falling late samples the fit succeeds, so the count of 4 is computed and never looked at.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 15, 'The stretch the engine discarded on the teaching well fits at a slope of -0.749171775150 with a fit quality of 0.999955540052 as a fraction. What is the honest way to report that beside the verdict the engine gave?', '["As the correct reading, since 0.999955540052 is the tighter line and fit quality is how the module chooses between candidate mechanisms.", "As a reading that fails coningSlope of -0.1 and therefore supports the channelling verdict from the other direction.", "As proof that the history holds a second verdict that the return object has no field for, not as a better reading, since four samples over 0.192990101 log cycles would not clear a span gate of 0.4.", "As a rounding artefact, because a fit through four points can always be made to look perfect."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both lines are excellent and they point opposite ways, and 0.998513658 was quoted as the evidence for the one that got printed.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 16, 'The engine holds a sentence saying that a ratio which has turned back down is itself the coning signature. Where does that sentence get printed?', '["On every coning verdict, which is why the published coning history returns it at confidence high, with that same sentence appended to its own note over the 20 late samples.", "Only where the derivative fit failed or every late derivative is within 1e-12 of zero, which on a turned history means only when no rising sample survives at all.", "Whenever the count of falling samples exceeds a quarter of the window, which is the ambiguous band applied to a count, and 4 falling out of 19 late samples clears a quarter.", "On any reading where the ratio slope and the derivative slope disagree in sign."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The teaching demonstration that reaches it has 8 late samples and every one of them falling, which is the arrangement the branch needs.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 17, 'One return object reports two slopes, 1.040602176 and 1.442132492, a gap of 0.401530316. What produced the gap?', '["The two fits sit on different data: the ratio slope was taken over all 19 late samples and the derivative slope over only the 15 whose derivative was positive.", "The derivative is the logarithmic derivative of the ratio, so on a power law it is always steeper by the exponent, so the gap of 0.401530316 is the exponent itself.", "The ratio fit was taken on the raw history and the derivative fit on the late window, which is why the second is steeper, over the full 38 samples against 19 late ones.", "Ordinary least squares was used for one and Theil-Sen for the other, and the two estimators separate on a curved trend."]'::jsonb, 0, 'On a power law the two would be equal, which is exactly the module''s own concession about why the boundary carries so much weight.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 18, 'The window sweep on the teaching well returns nine derivative slopes. Which single row is nearest to reversing the spend, and what does it rest on?', '["The row at 0.20, where the slope of 1.600276347 sits furthest above the threshold and the mechanism is indeterminate, so the spend has nowhere to go.", "The row at 0.50, where the reading is 0.142132492 from the boundary and the ambiguous flag comes back true, which is the module''s own warning.", "The row at 0.80, where the slope of 1.292632524 sits 0.007367476 below channellingSlope and the water shutoff comes back blocked.", "The row at 1.00, where the whole history is read and the slope reaches its flattest value of 1.229355999, over the whole history with no window."]'::jsonb, 2, 'One dial step from 0.70 to 0.80 crosses 1.3 and turns a candidate with three reasons into a categorical block.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 19, 'Somebody types a lateFraction of 2.50 by mistake. What does the module do about it?', '["It refuses, since the fraction is outside the range the argument is documented over, and returns the same error text it prints for a history of fewer than six producing samples.", "It reads the whole 38 sample history from t = 15.000000 days and returns a derivative slope of 1.229355999, with nothing on the result saying the request was trimmed.", "It returns the default reading at 0.5, because an out of range fraction falls back to the default, which is the documented fallback behaviour of the default fraction of 0.5.", "It reads the history two and a half times, which is why the slope comes back flatter than at 1.00."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The ceiling is 1.00 and the floor is 0.10, both applied in silence, and 0.05 and 0.10 are indistinguishable on the result for the same reason.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 20, 'Across the window sweep derivativeR2 and worR2 move in opposite directions. What does that make of the idea of choosing a window by fit quality?', '["It makes it a choice of which number to read first: the derivative fit ranks the narrow windows best and the ratio fit ranks the wide ones best, and the two rankings buy opposite verdicts.", "It makes it reliable, because a window that scores well on both is what the sweep is looking for and 0.50 is that window.", "It makes it impossible, because the module refuses any reading where the two fit qualities disagree by more than the ambiguous band, which on this history they do at every single row of the sweep.", "It makes no difference, since both quantities are fractions and the classifier reads neither of them."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At 0.20 the pair is 0.999944740 and 0.063449269, and at 1.00 it is 0.994988494 and 0.989832434.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 21, 'A golden series comes back from the classifier at confidence high with the ambiguous flag false, off a late derivative fit that is a perfectly straight unit line. What should a reader do with that verdict?', '["Treat it as the strongest verdict in the golden, since it is the only one the oracle publishes an expected mechanism for.", "Treat it as a refusal, because a fit quality of exactly 1.000000000 means the history was synthesised and cannot be diagnosed, so the classifier ought to decline to label it at all here.", "Treat it as ambiguous anyway, because 1.000000000 sits inside the band running from 1.050000 to 1.550000.", "Treat it as the case that most needs the plot and a person, because a slope of one is where displacement and channelling are least separable and the flag has nothing to say about it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The band catches a reading 0.142132492 from the boundary and misses this one, which is the harder case by the oracle''s own docstring.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 22, 'The flat branch prints that the ratio is sitting flat at a value taken from the final sample. What is in the same object that a reader should check against it?', '["The derivative fit, which is the quantity the branch actually tested and the one that would confirm or refute the sentence, if it had been fitted at all.", "The ratio fit: on the teaching gas history worSlope 0.356090047430 at worR2 0.949579197952 describes a ratio climbing steadily over 26 clean samples.", "The window start, which says how much of the history the sentence was written about.", "The confidence, which comes back n/a on this branch and is the module''s way of marking an unchecked assertion, and is carried out to the caller unchanged."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The condition is entirely about the derivative and the sentence is entirely about the ratio, and the fields disagree with the sentence beside them.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 23, 'Why did the flat branch survive review with an assertion nothing in it checks?', '["The only series it was exercised on really was flat: on the published flat history every ratio is 1.200000000, the ratio fit returns 0.000000000, and even the number in the sentence is right by accident.", "Because the note is not part of the returned object and therefore is not covered by any test.", "Because the branch fires only on histories with a missing derivative column, which no published case has.", "Because the oracle publishes an expected note for the flat history and the sentence matches it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'One honest series is all it takes for an assertion and the fields beside it never to disagree.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 24, 'A pipeline needs one condition that keeps a fails-open reading out of a screening. What should it test?', '["That ok is true and the confidence is not n/a, which separates the flat branch from every other reading, since the flat branch is the only path in the classifier that leaves the confidence field unset.", "That worR2 clears minR2 of 0.5, since a ratio fit that poor is what a coerced column produces.", "That the note does not contain the word flat, which is the one string the branch prints.", "That derivativeSlope, derivativeR2 and spanDecades are all present, because a named mechanism arriving with all three missing reached a verdict without fitting the series the verdict is about."]'::jsonb, 3, 'On the teaching gas history those three come back n/a while worSlope 0.356090047 and worR2 0.949579198 are both healthy.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 25, 'skinPiMultiplier accepts an after-skin of -7.361 on the teaching geometry and refuses -7.400. What has ok = true established at -7.361?', '["That the design sits inside the range the refusal text calls achievable, which is what the guard checks before returning, against the range of about -3 to -5 on acid.", "Only that the arithmetic did not divide by zero: the denominator after is 0.000728083 and the multiplier of 20412.125808300 comes back with no warning and no note.", "That the after-skin clears the floor by a margin the module considers safe, which is why no warning is attached, the floor for this particular geometry being -7.361728083308.", "That the multiplier is finite, which is the plausibility test skinFromPiRatio also performs on a claimed uplift."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The floor of -7.361728083308 is returned inside that very result and is compared against nothing but zero.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 26, 'The refusal text names real treatments reaching about -3 to -5 on acid and -5 to -6 on a fracture. What does the module do with those numbers?', '["Uses them as the guard, which is why an after-skin of -6.800 is refused with that sentence, along with everything below a fracture limit of -6.", "Compares them against minimumSkin, and refuses whichever of the two is shallower on the geometry supplied, and reports which one fired.", "Prints them, and nothing else: everything from -6 down to the pole is accepted with ok = true and no warning, no note and no flag.", "Attaches them to the result as a warning whenever the requested after-skin falls below the shallower of the two ranges, naming the range in the text."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At an after-skin of -6.800 the multiplier is 26.457156986 against 6.292734624 at -5.000, a factor of 4.204397383 with the same confidence attached.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 27, 'A vendor''s claimed uplift is audited through skinFromPiRatio on the teaching geometry and comes back at -6.495642426. What has the function told you about the claim?', '["Nothing beyond the arithmetic: it has no ok flag, no warning list and no plausibility check, and it returns the same shape of answer on both sides of the limit the module''s own text names.", "That the claim is impossible, since -6.495642426 is past the deepest fracture the refusal text describes.", "That the claim clears the geometry floor of -7.361728083 by a margin the function reports alongside the skin, which is the plausibility check this module performs on every one of its inverse calls.", "That the claim is at the edge of what acid can reach, which is the comparison the function makes internally."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The audit is a comparison the caller writes: against the floor for the arithmetic and against about -3 to -5 or -5 to -6 for the physics.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 28, 'The derived sweep of the zero-variance guard across sample counts and values is quoted as being worse news than a guard that never fires. Why?', '["Because it fires only on values below 1, so a bare water-oil ratio reaches it and a gas-oil ratio in scf/stb never does.", "Because it fires on every case the golden publishes and on none of the teaching ones, so the coverage is exactly inverted, which is the reason no test ever caught it.", "Because it fires at n = 3 and n = 4 on every value tried, splits at n = 5, and mostly stops by n = 8, so it passes small tidy tests and fails on the real cases.", "Because it returns a fit quality of 1.000000e+0 where the ordinary formula would have returned a value under minR2 of 0.5, so the guard raises the reading rather than losing it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Dead code is harmless once known to be dead, and y = 2 keeps firing until n = 32 while y = 0.9 has already stopped at n = 5.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 29, 'A ratio rising exactly logarithmically comes back with the note that the fit explains only 0.0 percent of it. What is that sentence a statement about?', '["The series, which is genuinely too scattered to carry a slope, since the derivative fit returns 2.271680e-31.", "The fit, not the series: a fit quality measures how much of the spread in y a line accounts for, and this derivative has no spread at all, sitting at 0.900000000 at every sample.", "The window, since the late window from 225.767578 days is too short for the fit to explain anything.", "The threshold, since minR2 of 0.5 is held as a fraction and 0.0 percent is the same number in the other scale, so the message and the default disagree with each other only in presentation."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The refusal is right about its own test and wrong about the well, and the vocabulary of noise is what invites the swap.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 30, 'A history climbs to 20.279117393 and its final row is a post-shut-in test of 0.060000000. What does the diagnosis look like, and what is the tell?', '["Mechanism displacement with ok = true and confidence n/a, and the tell is the object: no slope, no fit quality, no span and no window start, because minWor short-circuited the reading on that one row.", "Mechanism indeterminate with ok = false, and the tell is the error text about six producing samples.", "Mechanism displacement at confidence low with a slope of 1.150000000, and the tell is the ambiguous flag.", "Mechanism coning, and the tell is that the last ratio fell, which is the coning field test."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Restore that single sample and the same call returns worSlope 1.150000000 over a span of 0.872304180 log cycles from 268.364963 days.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 31, 'minWor is 0.1 and is compared against the last sample alone. What does the module''s own gas reasoning do about that?', '["It rescales the threshold by the expected gas-oil ratio of 950 scf/stb before the comparison is made.", "It refuses to hand a gas history to the diagnostic, which is why the gas shutoff verdict stops at consider.", "It applies the gate to the mean of the series instead, since a gas-oil ratio has no meaningful final reading.", "Nothing: it sends the user to run the diagnostic on the gas-oil ratio, where a last reading of 2151.864192 scf/stb clears a threshold of 0.1 by a factor of 2.151864e+4 and the gate does nothing at all."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A threshold named and documented for a water-oil ratio is applied unchanged to whatever column arrived.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 32, 'The gas shutoff gate is a factor of two on the expected ratio. What does the derived sweep show happening across it?', '["The verdict moves gradually from no through marginal to consider as the ratio climbs from 900 to 3000 scf/stb, with the reason count rising along with it.", "The verdict never moves, because the gas shutoff is gated on the mechanism rather than on the ratio, which is the only field the screening actually reads.", "1899 scf/stb against an expected 950 returns no with one reason at a ratio of 1.998947368, and 1900 returns consider with two at exactly 2.000000000.", "The verdict moves at 2152 scf/stb, the teaching well''s own value, which is the lowest ratio in the sweep returning consider, and the well''s own operating point."]'::jsonb, 2, 'One scf/stb decides between a verdict with one reason and a verdict with two, and the second reason is the instruction to run the diagnostic on the gas.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 33, 'Across a skin sweep from -4.000 to 20.000 and again with no skin entered, the hydraulic fracture verdict never moves. What does that establish about the branch?', '["That the skin is not the field it is gated on, and the water cut of 74.5 percent decides it instead, through the reason about growing out of zone.", "That its skin test decides nothing, because both arms of the ternary return the same string and the reason count stays at 3 on every row.", "That the verdict is correct, since a fracture pays on tight rock whether or not there is damage, which is exactly what its second reason says.", "That the branch is unreachable, and consider is the default the ranking applies to a treatment nothing was said about, in the absence of a gate."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The row with no skin entered is the proof: the test takes its other arm and the answer is the same word, while matrix acid on the same field returns unknown.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 34, 'The module writes three verdicts correctly on the same fields the fracture branch reads. What do they show?', '["That it knows how to write the branch: matrix acid is candidate above a skin of 2, marginal above 0, no at or below 0 and unknown with nothing entered, recompletion is candidate on channelling or a skin above 8, and artificial lift is consider above 70 percent water.", "That the fracture branch is the only one gated on skin, so its behaviour cannot be compared with any neighbour.", "That every verdict in the function is gated on the mechanism id, which is why they move together.", "That the thresholds are all overridable, which is why the fracture branch was left with a placeholder."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Three working gates in one function is exactly why the fracture defect survived review, since it shares their shape and their call signature.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 35, 'The oracle is described as good as far as it goes. What does it establish, and by what routes?', '["That the classifier agrees with a published mechanism on each of the four labelled histories, by comparison against the golden, which publishes an expected label for each of them.", "That the module''s refusals print the wording the golden publishes for each error path, so a changed message would fail the suite the way a changed slope would fail the arithmetic check.", "That two pure arithmetic functions are right, by Theil-Sen against ordinary least squares and by a radial Darcy rate built in SI against a ratio of two dimensionless groups.", "That the ranked order matches a published ordering for the seven treatments on each history."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On the published power law the slope of 1.350000000000 comes back with a difference of 0.0000e+0, and the five skin pairs agree to -8.8818e-16 at worst.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 36, 'On the published coning history the two routes disagree in the second decimal on data with no noise in it. What is the correct reading of that disagreement?', '["The engine is wrong, since the published Theil-Sen slope of -0.555098339661 is the committed value and the difference of 1.514312e-2 is an error.", "The oracle is wrong, since a fit quality of 0.948751314445 shows the engine''s line describes the points well.", "The history is noisy, which is why the published value carries a tolerance and the two routes cannot agree exactly.", "Both are right about their own question: the shape is not a power law, so ordinary least squares and Theil-Sen weight a curved log-log trend differently, and neither is measuring a slope that exists."]'::jsonb, 3, 'On the published power law the same two routes agree to 0.0000e+0, so the separation is about the shape and not about either estimator.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 37, 'Handed no diagnosis at all, the screening blocks the water shutoff. Why is that block the one the module gets right?', '["Because it is the only block the golden publishes an expected block reason for.", "Because it names its own uncertainty: it says the mechanism has not been established and that this history does not say which it is, and asks for more history or a production log.", "Because it is issued at confidence high, which is what separates it from the blocks issued on a diagnosis.", "Because it leaves the verdict unknown rather than blocked, which is the honest value for a field that was not supplied, which is what matrix acid returns when no skin was entered at all."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The water cut of 75 percent is called worth acting on and the refusal is about not knowing what is bringing the water.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 38, 'Which pairing in this module puts a correctly computed field directly alongside a printed sentence that contradicts it?', '["The coning history, where the engine slope of -0.539955222223 disagrees with the published -0.555098339661 in the second decimal.", "The constant-derivative demonstration, where a fit quality of 2.271680e-31 is reported as 0.0 percent in the note.", "The flat branch on a climbing gas history: the sentence says the ratio is sitting flat at 2151.86 while worSlope 0.356090047 and worR2 0.949579198 in the same object say it climbed by a factor of 2.248088575.", "The skin refusal, where a request at -7.400 is compared against a floor printed as -7.4."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The arithmetic functions are asserted to machine precision and the sentence-producing functions are asserted against nothing.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 39, 'What separates the run of the teaching well that reports channelling from the run that reports displacement, on identical data?', '["The derivative column, since one run reads it and the other reads a column of nulls, which then reads as a derivative of zero throughout.", "The window fraction alone: at 0.5 the derivative slope is 1.442132492 and at 0.9 it is 1.254360095, across a threshold of 1.3.", "The ratio gate, since one run has a last sample above minWor of 0.1 and the other does not, so one is short-circuited on a single row.", "The estimator, since one run is fitted by ordinary least squares and the other by Theil-Sen, which separate on a curved log-log trend."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Not one datum changes between the two, and the water shutoff moves from candidate with three reasons to blocked with one.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 40, 'Two failure contracts run through this module. Which functions use which, and what does a call site have to do about it?', '["skinPiMultiplier returns an object with an ok flag and an error string, while pssDenominator, minimumSkin and skinFromPiRatio return a bare NaN, so every call to the second kind needs Number.isFinite around it.", "chanDiagnosis returns an ok flag and screenTreatments returns a blocked flag, so a caller has to read both.", "The classifier returns strings and the arithmetic returns numbers, so a caller has to check the type of every return.", "All four skin functions return an ok flag, and the difference is only in the wording of the error each carries."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A bare NaN cannot be told from an answer, and the geometry that produces it is a wellbore radius larger than the drainage radius.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 41, 'A reading comes back with a mechanism, a confidence and a note, and a colleague asks how confident the module is that the label is right. What is the accurate answer?', '["That confidence high means the reading was checked against the published expected mechanism for that history shape, which the oracle publishes for all four of the labelled histories.", "That no part of the module makes that claim: the confidence field is assigned from the slope and the band, and no golden asserts any mechanism the classifier returns.", "That confidence tracks the fit quality, so a derivativeR2 of 0.998513658 is the module''s statement of certainty, and is carried straight across into the confidence field.", "That the ambiguous flag is the certainty, and a reading with the flag false has been confirmed against the plot."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The published displacement history comes back at confidence high with ambiguous false on a slope of exactly 1.000000000, and nothing checked it.'),
  ('intervention', 'advanced', 'final', null, 42, 'The full-history fit on the teaching well returns n = 38 having dropped none of the 38 handed to it. Why is that worth recording?', '["Because a point count equal to the rows supplied is what licenses reading the intercept as a coefficient.", "Because the golden asserts the point count on the raw fit, so it is the one number an independent route would catch.", "Because the fit drops what it cannot take a logarithm of without saying so, and the returned point count set against the rows supplied is the only record a caller can build of that.", "Because a window has not been applied yet, so the count is the only field that can differ from the count at the default window, where the derivative fit returns only 15 out of the 19 late samples."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On the same well at the default window the derivative fit returns 15 from a window of 19, and the drop is recorded nowhere.');
